-field/id #uuid "fa5bd8e0-2ab6-5b5d-9ae0-1984ba0dd1de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-218e5e71-f91e-5802-9d1e-930e6f48fbfc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-184d1ae0-19e7-5c7c-8ded-c578db9bcbbe" "answer-218e5e71-f91e-5802-9d1e-930e6f48fbfc" "answer-69e25e2d-c990-5056-83f7-f311ab076d1e" "answer-8b7c5160-9452-5c7b-acfb-3667157cb0dc" "answer-0fced1d8-7a6b-5da0-b804-3f55eb884ac9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-184d1ae0-19e7-5c7c-8ded-c578db9bcbbe" :answer/id #uuid "184d1ae0-19e7-5c7c-8ded-c578db9bcbbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-218e5e71-f91e-5802-9d1e-930e6f48fbfc" :answer/id #uuid "218e5e71-f91e-5802-9d1e-930e6f48fbfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69e25e2d-c990-5056-83f7-f311ab076d1e" :answer/id #uuid "69e25e2d-c990-5056-83f7-f311ab076d1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b7c5160-9452-5c7b-acfb-3667157cb0dc" :answer/id #uuid "8b7c5160-9452-5c7b-acfb-3667157cb0dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fced1d8-7a6b-5da0-b804-3f55eb884ac9" :answer/id #uuid "0fced1d8-7a6b-5da0-b804-3f55eb884ac9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f4ba96fb-f5da-576a-bf60-04d5aed6b6b9" :question/id #uuid "f4ba96fb-f5da-576a-bf60-04d5aed6b6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79162" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-2,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-2,3).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e890a07-e17a-5863-b90c-94cf5a71bd71"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e890a07-e17a-5863-b90c-94cf5a71bd71" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e890a07-e17a-5863-b90c-94cf5a71bd71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d5601ebf-64d3-57d5-9b2b-8c6dc6668b2d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-59047d6b-51e1-5284-81e2-22af3b1b3bc8" "answer-3040d2a4-9f6c-599b-8adb-eb56d4882741" "answer-284890cd-138a-5c4c-bd2c-22067d545fa7" "answer-d5601ebf-64d3-57d5-9b2b-8c6dc6668b2d" "answer-8f7bc84b-5364-5854-bc8c-3757da479f01"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59047d6b-51e1-5284-81e2-22af3b1b3bc8" :answer/id #uuid "59047d6b-51e1-5284-81e2-22af3b1b3bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3040d2a4-9f6c-599b-8adb-eb56d4882741" :answer/id #uuid "3040d2a4-9f6c-599b-8adb-eb56d4882741" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-284890cd-138a-5c4c-bd2c-22067d545fa7" :answer/id #uuid "284890cd-138a-5c4c-bd2c-22067d545fa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5601ebf-64d3-57d5-9b2b-8c6dc6668b2d" :answer/id #uuid "d5601ebf-64d3-57d5-9b2b-8c6dc6668b2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f7bc84b-5364-5854-bc8c-3757da479f01" :answer/id #uuid "8f7bc84b-5364-5854-bc8c-3757da479f01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7d806c10-53ec-5b39-8560-fe07bced1d84" :question/id #uuid "7d806c10-53ec-5b39-8560-fe07bced1d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-321945" :question/problem "![](images/79/795d51d5695c48f5d12c9057030f6a48e58a579fb262a83afc132425ec7668b3.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/a7/a78203eda3869c4478a97da0875dda7e72717911a6e1aee524bb963533a5e12e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ac9930bf-5836-5c06-b232-497097b50ff5" "answer-field-03df2f8e-3e63-5aea-98dc-2a4a0017437a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ac9930bf-5836-5c06-b232-497097b50ff5" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac9930bf-5836-5c06-b232-497097b50ff5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ace4a688-90ad-5fc0-a3eb-fc362a726947" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ace4a688-90ad-5fc0-a3eb-fc362a726947"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ace4a688-90ad-5fc0-a3eb-fc362a726947" :answer/id #uuid "ace4a688-90ad-5fc0-a3eb-fc362a726947" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-03df2f8e-3e63-5aea-98dc-2a4a0017437a" :answer-field/id #uuid "03df2f8e-3e63-5aea-98dc-2a4a0017437a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5bd82803-6b71-55a2-8f5a-355333a844cb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5bd82803-6b71-55a2-8f5a-355333a844cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bd82803-6b71-55a2-8f5a-355333a844cb" :answer/id #uuid "5bd82803-6b71-55a2-8f5a-355333a844cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5e3d47e1-6723-5134-bd1d-2937862c014c" :question/id #uuid "5e3d47e1-6723-5134-bd1d-2937862c014c" :question/math-academy-id "q-321957" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca3a73d411d7b766196a2bebf56f9d678aba54a4f2265ac08c36d0fb3fbf03b0.png)\n\nThe arc of the circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/37/37665aa7ea5388a1162ca68a1662aaa86d6369f083d7563dbf27c521c13a7c72.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-20006ed3-e230-529e-81fb-8b1bee54d924" "answer-field-7156cc32-810c-50dc-a3b4-4e2b4679767b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-20006ed3-e230-529e-81fb-8b1bee54d924" :answer-field/id #uuid "20006ed3-e230-529e-81fb-8b1bee54d924" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7988efe9-2597-5390-9e57-33004a453a49" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7988efe9-2597-5390-9e57-33004a453a49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7988efe9-2597-5390-9e57-33004a453a49" :answer/id #uuid "7988efe9-2597-5390-9e57-33004a453a49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7156cc32-810c-50dc-a3b4-4e2b4679767b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7156cc32-810c-50dc-a3b4-4e2b4679767b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-98a5631a-e7cc-5569-afb2-d11edaf4836d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98a5631a-e7cc-5569-afb2-d11edaf4836d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98a5631a-e7cc-5569-afb2-d11edaf4836d" :answer/id #uuid "98a5631a-e7cc-5569-afb2-d11edaf4836d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1d2632a-4694-5c22-b050-b15669bba222" :question/id #uuid "b1d2632a-4694-5c22-b050-b15669bba222" :question/math-academy-id "q-79167" :question/problem "![](images/15/158ce9317d062a090ed36bda7361d025e4c8f4c68ea43484f50015f01c411a30.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5c/5cc99d78c5fe5afacde4ba96e2a4010b0fab66bdabbe5c2b518bd936f36c7d84.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5908b398-7b43-5b62-978f-3d17f21f0446"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5908b398-7b43-5b62-978f-3d17f21f0446" :answer-field/id #uuid "5908b398-7b43-5b62-978f-3d17f21f0446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a28191ad-4a5a-576f-bfda-4f31c85b6f6b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6ecba329-64f6-5352-93a3-b9c05b3611c6" "answer-4a1fae89-5862-5273-b352-1b55c259e49d" "answer-a28191ad-4a5a-576f-bfda-4f31c85b6f6b" "answer-4c7dc81c-912e-5bf3-988e-249f3367e151" "answer-98e0b29e-8625-5698-b82d-e8fb994ff0dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ecba329-64f6-5352-93a3-b9c05b3611c6" :answer/id #uuid "6ecba329-64f6-5352-93a3-b9c05b3611c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a1fae89-5862-5273-b352-1b55c259e49d" :answer/id #uuid "4a1fae89-5862-5273-b352-1b55c259e49d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a28191ad-4a5a-576f-bfda-4f31c85b6f6b" :answer/id #uuid "a28191ad-4a5a-576f-bfda-4f31c85b6f6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c7dc81c-912e-5bf3-988e-249f3367e151" :answer/id #uuid "4c7dc81c-912e-5bf3-988e-249f3367e151" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98e0b29e-8625-5698-b82d-e8fb994ff0dc" :answer/id #uuid "98e0b29e-8625-5698-b82d-e8fb994ff0dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-58130b5b-5f37-553e-8991-9f3dd2a84005" :question/id #uuid "58130b5b-5f37-553e-8991-9f3dd2a84005" :question/math-academy-id "q-106297" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-091e02c8-01f9-5997-be0f-efbf60f36431"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-091e02c8-01f9-5997-be0f-efbf60f36431" :answer-field/id #uuid "091e02c8-01f9-5997-be0f-efbf60f36431" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4760e9b3-e753-55b3-8549-49c79fed671e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-49a18dee-f7bf-5c98-846a-ecb30433c784" "answer-4760e9b3-e753-55b3-8549-49c79fed671e" "answer-26128e9c-9d9e-57af-b197-f06d328fb1d0" "answer-e11feadc-e5a5-59e9-aa5a-84838827e52d" "answer-b09c9317-b0eb-5d82-b83b-452a31eca1a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49a18dee-f7bf-5c98-846a-ecb30433c784" :answer/id #uuid "49a18dee-f7bf-5c98-846a-ecb30433c784" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4760e9b3-e753-55b3-8549-49c79fed671e" :answer/id #uuid "4760e9b3-e753-55b3-8549-49c79fed671e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26128e9c-9d9e-57af-b197-f06d328fb1d0" :answer/id #uuid "26128e9c-9d9e-57af-b197-f06d328fb1d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e11feadc-e5a5-59e9-aa5a-84838827e52d" :answer/id #uuid "e11feadc-e5a5-59e9-aa5a-84838827e52d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b09c9317-b0eb-5d82-b83b-452a31eca1a6" :answer/id #uuid "b09c9317-b0eb-5d82-b83b-452a31eca1a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5bcab4b1-53e9-547b-81a4-3bf0881c104a" :question/id #uuid "5bcab4b1-53e9-547b-81a4-3bf0881c104a" :question/math-academy-id "q-6891" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =16(1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c98d58f-3fec-5e1a-83a6-98106a7cf5a3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c98d58f-3fec-5e1a-83a6-98106a7cf5a3" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c98d58f-3fec-5e1a-83a6-98106a7cf5a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-644dced5-34e6-5f21-8852-dbfa2b44f065" :answer-field/choices ["answer-644dced5-34e6-5f21-8852-dbfa2b44f065" "answer-1312b130-f076-56dc-a6ca-175281e64d2f" "answer-9f759471-c9f9-5e4b-a90e-babe10376f0d" "answer-477bc9a6-005d-535c-b4ab-779587a92597" "answer-3090c984-5ed8-5035-ba5b-4c5acd082c78"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-644dced5-34e6-5f21-8852-dbfa2b44f065" :answer/id #uuid "644dced5-34e6-5f21-8852-dbfa2b44f065" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1312b130-f076-56dc-a6ca-175281e64d2f" :answer/id #uuid "1312b130-f076-56dc-a6ca-175281e64d2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f759471-c9f9-5e4b-a90e-babe10376f0d" :answer/id #uuid "9f759471-c9f9-5e4b-a90e-babe10376f0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-477bc9a6-005d-535c-b4ab-779587a92597" :answer/id #uuid "477bc9a6-005d-535c-b4ab-779587a92597" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3090c984-5ed8-5035-ba5b-4c5acd082c78" :answer/id #uuid "3090c984-5ed8-5035-ba5b-4c5acd082c78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d2f4bec-f2bc-5622-80f4-e5a5ebaa94bf" :question/id #uuid "3d2f4bec-f2bc-5622-80f4-e5a5ebaa94bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-321990" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(x+2) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(y+3) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{36}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fb5a8c81-2643-51d7-9805-3937d851aee1" "answer-field-f62681d9-cd8d-595e-ba4f-a4722ad371ab" "answer-field-9908f8c2-74fa-5229-9b2a-2eddd163b590"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb5a8c81-2643-51d7-9805-3937d851aee1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb5a8c81-2643-51d7-9805-3937d851aee1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-eb24c4f0-6957-5fc0-b36b-ba0d18bb9054" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eb24c4f0-6957-5fc0-b36b-ba0d18bb9054"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb24c4f0-6957-5fc0-b36b-ba0d18bb9054" :answer/id #uuid "eb24c4f0-6957-5fc0-b36b-ba0d18bb9054" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f62681d9-cd8d-595e-ba4f-a4722ad371ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "f62681d9-cd8d-595e-ba4f-a4722ad371ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b5315806-a439-585f-85b6-8fe87007f878" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b5315806-a439-585f-85b6-8fe87007f878"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5315806-a439-585f-85b6-8fe87007f878" :answer/id #uuid "b5315806-a439-585f-85b6-8fe87007f878" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9908f8c2-74fa-5229-9b2a-2eddd163b590" :answer-field/id #uuid "9908f8c2-74fa-5229-9b2a-2eddd163b590" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-40c364b9-d7be-545b-8bb2-ae86c4c7fcd2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-40c364b9-d7be-545b-8bb2-ae86c4c7fcd2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40c364b9-d7be-545b-8bb2-ae86c4c7fcd2" :answer/id #uuid "40c364b9-d7be-545b-8bb2-ae86c4c7fcd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088961 :tutorial/content "To convert the parametric equations of a circle to a Cartesian equation, we can eliminate the parameter $\\theta$ using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the circle defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=5\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^2+y^2 = 25.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087894 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166755 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fd040bd9-e3ef-5708-a352-cc4c61514e23" "question-727d2976-e027-5f8e-a059-0575427eb806"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd040bd9-e3ef-5708-a352-cc4c61514e23" :question/id #uuid "fd040bd9-e3ef-5708-a352-cc4c61514e23" :question/math-academy-id "q-96426" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(2x-1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(2x-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x-1)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (2x-1)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(4{x}^{2}-4x+1)-1}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x(x-1)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d51b538-39cd-589a-b246-54b69d35dae4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d51b538-39cd-589a-b246-54b69d35dae4" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d51b538-39cd-589a-b246-54b69d35dae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ba4ffdb-ed17-5ed7-ac4b-60e5e8212467" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ece0ec44-e760-591f-b411-d7660b6288df" "answer-9ba4ffdb-ed17-5ed7-ac4b-60e5e8212467" "answer-37c7d91d-cc06-508e-8136-0c7783921fdf" "answer-b515d758-ad04-57d6-957e-d01e00b0efbc" "answer-813bb0df-60a0-5317-aae6-74937157ce77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ece0ec44-e760-591f-b411-d7660b6288df" :answer/id #uuid "ece0ec44-e760-591f-b411-d7660b6288df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x(2x-1)}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ba4ffdb-ed17-5ed7-ac4b-60e5e8212467" :answer/id #uuid "9ba4ffdb-ed17-5ed7-ac4b-60e5e8212467" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x(x-1)}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37c7d91d-cc06-508e-8136-0c7783921fdf" :answer/id #uuid "37c7d91d-cc06-508e-8136-0c7783921fdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x(x-2)}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b515d758-ad04-57d6-957e-d01e00b0efbc" :answer/id #uuid "b515d758-ad04-57d6-957e-d01e00b0efbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x(2x-1)}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-813bb0df-60a0-5317-aae6-74937157ce77" :answer/id #uuid "813bb0df-60a0-5317-aae6-74937157ce77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x(x-1)}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-727d2976-e027-5f8e-a059-0575427eb806" :question/id #uuid "727d2976-e027-5f8e-a059-0575427eb806" :question/math-academy-id "q-341959" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=4\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(3x-4),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(4\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(3x-4)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(3x-4)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(3x-4)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (3x-4)^{′} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(3x-4)^{2}-1}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{(3x-4)^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-114d9c4c-5ec1-5cf1-a611-dbc9b12d3763"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-114d9c4c-5ec1-5cf1-a611-dbc9b12d3763" :answer-field/id #uuid "114d9c4c-5ec1-5cf1-a611-dbc9b12d3763" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-33841734-1cb4-596a-90ad-9968f0fc8c0f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33841734-1cb4-596a-90ad-9968f0fc8c0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33841734-1cb4-596a-90ad-9968f0fc8c0f" :answer/id #uuid "33841734-1cb4-596a-90ad-9968f0fc8c0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{(3x-4)^{2}-1}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166756 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-973108cd-4a12-5f7e-8def-c33e7591ffaa" "question-0bc32c07-1675-5c17-a61b-a851fe6dbaf3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-973108cd-4a12-5f7e-8def-c33e7591ffaa" :question/id #uuid "973108cd-4a12-5f7e-8def-c33e7591ffaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-342458" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\,x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\,x)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\,x)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{(\\sqrt{3}\\,x)}^{2}}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{3}\\,x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-3{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{3{x}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2cde32a2-c89a-582c-93af-924f59b3619d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2cde32a2-c89a-582c-93af-924f59b3619d" :answer-field/id #uuid "2cde32a2-c89a-582c-93af-924f59b3619d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f3ea6a3a-8078-5e78-9e2e-c7ecb759b2d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f3ea6a3a-8078-5e78-9e2e-c7ecb759b2d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3ea6a3a-8078-5e78-9e2e-c7ecb759b2d2" :answer/id #uuid "f3ea6a3a-8078-5e78-9e2e-c7ecb759b2d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{1-3x^2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0bc32c07-1675-5c17-a61b-a851fe6dbaf3" :question/id #uuid "0bc32c07-1675-5c17-a61b-a851fe6dbaf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-342471" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{5},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $5{x}^{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{({x}^{5})}^{2}}\\cdot {({x}^{5})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{10}}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{1-{x}^{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-caf30c59-3266-50ba-a4bd-cf968c8851d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-caf30c59-3266-50ba-a4bd-cf968c8851d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "caf30c59-3266-50ba-a4bd-cf968c8851d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e2bbc451-3971-5718-9b6a-c792cbf3baa2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e2bbc451-3971-5718-9b6a-c792cbf3baa2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2bbc451-3971-5718-9b6a-c792cbf3baa2" :answer/id #uuid "e2bbc451-3971-5718-9b6a-c792cbf3baa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-{x}^{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166757 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-40534571-9b68-5a71-b2eb-96fad07c5254"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-40534571-9b68-5a71-b2eb-96fad07c5254" :question/id #uuid "40534571-9b68-5a71-b2eb-96fad07c5254" :question/math-academy-id "q-342666" :question/problem "$$\n\nCalculate ${g}^{′}(x)$ for $g(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3).$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $={e}^{x}\\,\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)\\,+$ | $4{e}^{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAnd the product rule is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}(u\\cdot v)}{\\text{d}x}={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, chain rule, and  formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)+{e}^{x}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3))}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)+{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(4x+3)^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(4x+3)}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)+{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(4x+3)^{2}+1}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\,\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)+\\frac{4{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{(4x+3)^{2}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-958fb880-28de-5a44-b1da-b802306a2223"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-958fb880-28de-5a44-b1da-b802306a2223" :answer-field/id #uuid "958fb880-28de-5a44-b1da-b802306a2223" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-86653cab-377d-5ed8-a471-e37ef1bae183" :answer-field/choices ["answer-86653cab-377d-5ed8-a471-e37ef1bae183"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86653cab-377d-5ed8-a471-e37ef1bae183" :answer/id #uuid "86653cab-377d-5ed8-a471-e37ef1bae183" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+(4x+3)^2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fe9eb0bd-24ad-5960-8681-02004f47daf0" :activity/id #uuid "fe9eb0bd-24ad-5960-8681-02004f47daf0" :activity/title "Differentiating Inverse Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049314 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-f9309d58-72f3-5655-bced-f00af4482162" "step-cefc5e76-e5ef-5be3-aef8-c08aca913b13" "step-20ca1f8e-33d8-5d92-9a68-9b271fbbb6eb" "step-39fef160-1d58-52ed-94e8-3b82d441f7ea" "step-670e1922-ccd4-5f3d-9789-2c3188ece2f5"] :activity/first-step "step-f9309d58-72f3-5655-bced-f00af4482162" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f9309d58-72f3-5655-bced-f00af4482162" :step/id #uuid "f9309d58-72f3-5655-bced-f00af4482162" :step/content "question-fd040bd9-e3ef-5708-a352-cc4c61514e23" :step/next "step-cefc5e76-e5ef-5be3-aef8-c08aca913b13" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cefc5e76-e5ef-5be3-aef8-c08aca913b13" :step/id #uuid "cefc5e76-e5ef-5be3-aef8-c08aca913b13" :step/content "question-727d2976-e027-5f8e-a059-0575427eb806" :step/next "step-20ca1f8e-33d8-5d92-9a68-9b271fbbb6eb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-20ca1f8e-33d8-5d92-9a68-9b271fbbb6eb" :step/id #uuid "20ca1f8e-33d8-5d92-9a68-9b271fbbb6eb" :step/content "question-973108cd-4a12-5f7e-8def-c33e7591ffaa" :step/next "step-39fef160-1d58-52ed-94e8-3b82d441f7ea" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-39fef160-1d58-52ed-94e8-3b82d441f7ea" :step/id #uuid "39fef160-1d58-52ed-94e8-3b82d441f7ea" :step/content "question-0bc32c07-1675-5c17-a61b-a851fe6dbaf3" :step/next "step-670e1922-ccd4-5f3d-9789-2c3188ece2f5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-670e1922-ccd4-5f3d-9789-2c3188ece2f5" :step/id #uuid "670e1922-ccd4-5f3d-9789-2c3188ece2f5" :step/content "question-40534571-9b68-5a71-b2eb-96fad07c5254" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054740 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-860910b4-2f3e-5abd-a759-661586cb6055" "question-bdbe7587-32bb-56f0-8c5c-492c8b22516f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-860910b4-2f3e-5abd-a759-661586cb6055" :question/id #uuid "860910b4-2f3e-5abd-a759-661586cb6055" :question/math-academy-id "q-277907" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the roots of $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}-4t-21}{{t}^{2}+7t+10}$ are\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$\nand\n$\\,\\,t=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The roots of $f(t)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(t)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{t}^{2}-4t-21}{{t}^{2}+7t+10}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor $f(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-4t-21}{{t}^{2}+7t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-7)(t+3)}{(t+5)(t+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-7)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =7,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(t)=0$, in ascending order, are $t=-3$ and $t=7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0ac2b473-8562-5640-a093-bef975d33359" "answer-field-ac9d0efb-0c6f-57c1-96e7-540134465667"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0ac2b473-8562-5640-a093-bef975d33359" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac2b473-8562-5640-a093-bef975d33359" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c8fa7c33-0920-5c80-b81d-66783a8901b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c8fa7c33-0920-5c80-b81d-66783a8901b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8fa7c33-0920-5c80-b81d-66783a8901b6" :answer/id #uuid "c8fa7c33-0920-5c80-b81d-66783a8901b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ac9d0efb-0c6f-57c1-96e7-540134465667" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac9d0efb-0c6f-57c1-96e7-540134465667" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f64427b9-45e9-556f-9926-61f1f8b1c91c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f64427b9-45e9-556f-9926-61f1f8b1c91c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f64427b9-45e9-556f-9926-61f1f8b1c91c" :answer/id #uuid "f64427b9-45e9-556f-9926-61f1f8b1c91c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bdbe7587-32bb-56f0-8c5c-492c8b22516f" :question/id #uuid "bdbe7587-32bb-56f0-8c5c-492c8b22516f" :question/math-academy-id "q-277916" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the roots of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-8}{{x}^{2}-9x+18}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$\nand\n$\\,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The roots of $f(x)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(x)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-8}{{x}^{2}-9x+18}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-8}{{x}^{2}-9x+18} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+4)(x-2)}{(x-3)(x-6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(x)=0$, in ascending order, are $x=-4$ and $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b91aa5d-2ec5-510f-9b6f-4340499a244c" "answer-field-844d3128-113d-58e2-9969-6c438840e1d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b91aa5d-2ec5-510f-9b6f-4340499a244c" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b91aa5d-2ec5-510f-9b6f-4340499a244c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-60aaf0db-8e49-5cec-affb-68ab9d86ab91" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60aaf0db-8e49-5cec-affb-68ab9d86ab91"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60aaf0db-8e49-5cec-affb-68ab9d86ab91" :answer/id #uuid "60aaf0db-8e49-5cec-affb-68ab9d86ab91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-844d3128-113d-58e2-9969-6c438840e1d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "844d3128-113d-58e2-9969-6c438840e1d9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d2f22493-6c20-57c1-8868-c03954a3bfa7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2f22493-6c20-57c1-8868-c03954a3bfa7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2f22493-6c20-57c1-8868-c03954a3bfa7" :answer/id #uuid "d2f22493-6c20-57c1-8868-c03954a3bfa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054741 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c691641a-bf97-5ba0-ae7f-e25ca5480f64"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c691641a-bf97-5ba0-ae7f-e25ca5480f64" :question/id #uuid "c691641a-bf97-5ba0-ae7f-e25ca5480f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-277942" :question/problem "Solve $f(t)=0,$ where $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}-5t+6}{3t-9}.$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-5t+6}{3t-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-3)(t-2)}{3(t-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(t-3)}(t-2)}{3\\cancel{(t-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-2)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-2 & =0\\,⟹\\,t=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(t)=0$ is $t=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-914487b6-8ecc-5985-adf5-223f2a382299"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-914487b6-8ecc-5985-adf5-223f2a382299" :answer-field/id #uuid "914487b6-8ecc-5985-adf5-223f2a382299" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d82c5625-6116-52bd-ab3d-67180f93a5e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d82c5625-6116-52bd-ab3d-67180f93a5e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d82c5625-6116-52bd-ab3d-67180f93a5e3" :answer/id #uuid "d82c5625-6116-52bd-ab3d-67180f93a5e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-200a51bf-b5ea-52f8-a788-0b94b86d7bb5" :activity/id #uuid "200a51bf-b5ea-52f8-a788-0b94b86d7bb5" :activity/title "Roots of Rational Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049931 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-c023283e-1f36-5014-9be3-65e009d532b9" "step-a40b2f4f-86ff-52ad-b41a-1114cd7904f4" "step-8d518c8f-e6ea-5ed3-96ad-151f7204d441"] :activity/first-step "step-c023283e-1f36-5014-9be3-65e009d532b9" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c023283e-1f36-5014-9be3-65e009d532b9" :step/id #uuid "c023283e-1f36-5014-9be3-65e009d532b9" :step/content "question-860910b4-2f3e-5abd-a759-661586cb6055" :step/next "step-a40b2f4f-86ff-52ad-b41a-1114cd7904f4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a40b2f4f-86ff-52ad-b41a-1114cd7904f4" :step/id #uuid "a40b2f4f-86ff-52ad-b41a-1114cd7904f4" :step/content "question-bdbe7587-32bb-56f0-8c5c-492c8b22516f" :step/next "step-8d518c8f-e6ea-5ed3-96ad-151f7204d441" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8d518c8f-e6ea-5ed3-96ad-151f7204d441" :step/id #uuid "8d518c8f-e6ea-5ed3-96ad-151f7204d441" :step/content "question-c691641a-bf97-5ba0-ae7f-e25ca5480f64" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059793 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-00bde4d7-2128-56ee-87f4-eb818e9cb4e3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059991 :question/problem "If $\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2}=1$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2} & =1 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)} & ={7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}=x+1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={7}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & =49 \\\\ x & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056304 :answer-field/correct 17592186056305 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233679 :question/problem "Find $x$ such that ${log}_{4}⁡(3x-2)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(3x-2) & =3 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3x-2)} & ={4}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3x-2)}=3x-2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2 & ={4}^{3} \\\\ 3x-2 & =64 \\\\ 3x & =66 \\\\ x & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2b66e190-3a44-5bcd-8fa8-92a479622c92"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2b66e190-3a44-5bcd-8fa8-92a479622c92" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b66e190-3a44-5bcd-8fa8-92a479622c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c020f3d7-85a1-5452-a318-b9aba6af4d51" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c020f3d7-85a1-5452-a318-b9aba6af4d51" "answer-51a1eea1-22ad-5e39-840b-a993eeed3ec9" "answer-e55c01a0-412d-5e01-991f-926ab7486bb7" "answer-48c860bd-e06a-5669-aacd-322bd3817f4d" "answer-13d98f75-ec98-55b8-bd6d-557ed859ed11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c020f3d7-85a1-5452-a318-b9aba6af4d51" :answer/id #uuid "c020f3d7-85a1-5452-a318-b9aba6af4d51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51a1eea1-22ad-5e39-840b-a993eeed3ec9" :answer/id #uuid "51a1eea1-22ad-5e39-840b-a993eeed3ec9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e55c01a0-412d-5e01-991f-926ab7486bb7" :answer/id #uuid "e55c01a0-412d-5e01-991f-926ab7486bb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{62}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48c860bd-e06a-5669-aacd-322bd3817f4d" :answer/id #uuid "48c860bd-e06a-5669-aacd-322bd3817f4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{68}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13d98f75-ec98-55b8-bd6d-557ed859ed11" :answer/id #uuid "13d98f75-ec98-55b8-bd6d-557ed859ed11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{83}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-00bde4d7-2128-56ee-87f4-eb818e9cb4e3" :question/id #uuid "00bde4d7-2128-56ee-87f4-eb818e9cb4e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-100626" :question/problem "If $-16{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)=-48$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =-48 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =\\frac{-48}{-16} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)} & ={7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)}=x-128.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-128 & ={7}^{3} \\\\ x-128 & =343 \\\\ x & =471.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-25a7aacd-0ade-5f57-a8d3-34286e4a41cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-25a7aacd-0ade-5f57-a8d3-34286e4a41cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "25a7aacd-0ade-5f57-a8d3-34286e4a41cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f3a6a46f-1b59-5281-b140-01c12fd66cf1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf067dd4-c8bc-5704-b97b-05088b2a487b" "answer-6f1ae7c7-2dee-55e1-8b00-d4531dcc5579" "answer-e8cde56b-bba5-5c32-a43d-c79e4c3cdd75" "answer-0bb7199e-65dd-5bd9-a79d-442fc232faa9" "answer-f3a6a46f-1b59-5281-b140-01c12fd66cf1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf067dd4-c8bc-5704-b97b-05088b2a487b" :answer/id #uuid "cf067dd4-c8bc-5704-b97b-05088b2a487b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "343" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f1ae7c7-2dee-55e1-8b00-d4531dcc5579" :answer/id #uuid "6f1ae7c7-2dee-55e1-8b00-d4531dcc5579" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "362" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8cde56b-bba5-5c32-a43d-c79e4c3cdd75" :answer/id #uuid "e8cde56b-bba5-5c32-a43d-c79e4c3cdd75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "371" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bb7199e-65dd-5bd9-a79d-442fc232faa9" :answer/id #uuid "0bb7199e-65dd-5bd9-a79d-442fc232faa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "425" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3a6a46f-1b59-5281-b140-01c12fd66cf1" :answer/id #uuid "f3a6a46f-1b59-5281-b140-01c12fd66cf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "471" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-76083efd-d525-5432-a337-c60866a16fa3" :activity/id #uuid "76083efd-d525-5432-a337-c60866a16fa3" :activity/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049944 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-9f12e949-c5a4-5db4-9f36-811356009a82" "step-2a4f9450-03d9-5f9e-9859-0889b3ec4d3f" "step-1c02ef41-09d7-5c72-a8d1-2766d66724c0"] :activity/first-step "step-9f12e949-c5a4-5db4-9f36-811356009a82" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9f12e949-c5a4-5db4-9f36-811356009a82" :step/id #uuid "9f12e949-c5a4-5db4-9f36-811356009a82" :step/content 17592186059991 :step/next "step-2a4f9450-03d9-5f9e-9859-0889b3ec4d3f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2a4f9450-03d9-5f9e-9859-0889b3ec4d3f" :step/id #uuid "2a4f9450-03d9-5f9e-9859-0889b3ec4d3f" :step/content 17592186233679 :step/next "step-1c02ef41-09d7-5c72-a8d1-2766d66724c0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1c02ef41-09d7-5c72-a8d1-2766d66724c0" :step/id #uuid "1c02ef41-09d7-5c72-a8d1-2766d66724c0" :step/content "question-00bde4d7-2128-56ee-87f4-eb818e9cb4e3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179675 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6f3c55a9-3e7c-5106-8273-98cee0c05243" "question-34262fe5-7916-5312-b653-79d60bb7bbe7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179729 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-198d2d03-309c-5424-a889-3994cff0e1c9" "question-d6062a1b-c189-5825-831a-1185f156e120"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179734 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-08e87b9c-9adc-5a8c-b060-dda37adbb915" "question-b34fc554-6133-5c96-98c9-93a11d4b7862"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6f3c55a9-3e7c-5106-8273-98cee0c05243" :question/id #uuid "6f3c55a9-3e7c-5106-8273-98cee0c05243" :question/math-academy-id "q-124870" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,i+j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+({sin}^{2}⁡(2t)-{1}^{2})\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0258956-1ae0-545c-833f-e715647fb6c5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0258956-1ae0-545c-833f-e715647fb6c5" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0258956-1ae0-545c-833f-e715647fb6c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-22201dff-61e9-5435-a6a4-ec1001f8fba7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9e5e15da-7973-535e-a572-2e011cbd5da9" "answer-efcdd02b-1a12-5093-8ddd-b0ba2d89d81e" "answer-968eea0b-a6c3-5719-af51-829806f3263f" "answer-22201dff-61e9-5435-a6a4-ec1001f8fba7" "answer-c3d52a07-3bbb-53b0-a0b0-9e85a23a30bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e5e15da-7973-535e-a572-2e011cbd5da9" :answer/id #uuid "9e5e15da-7973-535e-a572-2e011cbd5da9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efcdd02b-1a12-5093-8ddd-b0ba2d89d81e" :answer/id #uuid "efcdd02b-1a12-5093-8ddd-b0ba2d89d81e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-968eea0b-a6c3-5719-af51-829806f3263f" :answer/id #uuid "968eea0b-a6c3-5719-af51-829806f3263f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22201dff-61e9-5435-a6a4-ec1001f8fba7" :answer/id #uuid "22201dff-61e9-5435-a6a4-ec1001f8fba7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3d52a07-3bbb-53b0-a0b0-9e85a23a30bd" :answer/id #uuid "c3d52a07-3bbb-53b0-a0b0-9e85a23a30bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-34262fe5-7916-5312-b653-79d60bb7bbe7" :question/id #uuid "34262fe5-7916-5312-b653-79d60bb7bbe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124877" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15565180-33b3-52ce-9a0a-037fbe40360a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15565180-33b3-52ce-9a0a-037fbe40360a" :answer-field/id #uuid "15565180-33b3-52ce-9a0a-037fbe40360a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6afc72d8-8408-50d9-bca7-f953e40f36f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bae2108b-9562-5fdc-b2ed-c916f2046fc8" "answer-04a0d4eb-fefb-5e1d-8b8b-53d270e9ff12" "answer-6afc72d8-8408-50d9-bca7-f953e40f36f3" "answer-29ed2488-96ec-539f-848b-3a7f0c77834e" "answer-5642d196-fd16-5650-b8ce-2b73794cc067"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bae2108b-9562-5fdc-b2ed-c916f2046fc8" :answer/id #uuid "bae2108b-9562-5fdc-b2ed-c916f2046fc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04a0d4eb-fefb-5e1d-8b8b-53d270e9ff12" :answer/id #uuid "04a0d4eb-fefb-5e1d-8b8b-53d270e9ff12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6afc72d8-8408-50d9-bca7-f953e40f36f3" :answer/id #uuid "6afc72d8-8408-50d9-bca7-f953e40f36f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29ed2488-96ec-539f-848b-3a7f0c77834e" :answer/id #uuid "29ed2488-96ec-539f-848b-3a7f0c77834e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5642d196-fd16-5650-b8ce-2b73794cc067" :answer/id #uuid "5642d196-fd16-5650-b8ce-2b73794cc067" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-198d2d03-309c-5424-a889-3994cff0e1c9" :question/id #uuid "198d2d03-309c-5424-a889-3994cff0e1c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124837" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25{cos}^{2}⁡t-25{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9f0f87ed-2e6a-5c16-a67c-0ec64db6f138"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9f0f87ed-2e6a-5c16-a67c-0ec64db6f138" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0f87ed-2e6a-5c16-a67c-0ec64db6f138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-69837eb7-597e-5314-9105-3e7be9be3024" :answer-field/choices ["answer-69837eb7-597e-5314-9105-3e7be9be3024" "answer-236555c7-ee6e-5be6-bd74-3a41e1426296" "answer-08b00f42-9741-5f92-9339-a2a2a3e0a478" "answer-788fc73e-f6c0-5d4e-9563-fec8ac0a5b5d" "answer-443fcd61-e7db-54c9-9474-4eaf8ee080b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69837eb7-597e-5314-9105-3e7be9be3024" :answer/id #uuid "69837eb7-597e-5314-9105-3e7be9be3024" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-236555c7-ee6e-5be6-bd74-3a41e1426296" :answer/id #uuid "236555c7-ee6e-5be6-bd74-3a41e1426296" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+{e}^{t}\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08b00f42-9741-5f92-9339-a2a2a3e0a478" :answer/id #uuid "08b00f42-9741-5f92-9339-a2a2a3e0a478" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{e}^{t}\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-788fc73e-f6c0-5d4e-9563-fec8ac0a5b5d" :answer/id #uuid "788fc73e-f6c0-5d4e-9563-fec8ac0a5b5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-443fcd61-e7db-54c9-9474-4eaf8ee080b8" :answer/id #uuid "443fcd61-e7db-54c9-9474-4eaf8ee080b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d6062a1b-c189-5825-831a-1185f156e120" :question/id #uuid "d6062a1b-c189-5825-831a-1185f156e120" :question/math-academy-id "q-124866" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+yz\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the line $r(t)=t\\,i+5t\\,j+\\,k$ for $t\\in [2,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=5t,\\,z(t)=1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+5\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+yz\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}xy\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+yz\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(xy\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+yz\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}5{t}^{2}\\cdot (1)+5t\\cdot (5)+1\\cdot (0)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}5{t}^{2}+25t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b181f285-5443-572d-9a9e-98fe01481860"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b181f285-5443-572d-9a9e-98fe01481860" :answer-field/id #uuid "b181f285-5443-572d-9a9e-98fe01481860" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ef8374b-3c52-5aba-a47f-1d45b49a355d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e072df42-f8fc-5db8-a2ff-18aa9b23b0c3" "answer-0ef8374b-3c52-5aba-a47f-1d45b49a355d" "answer-f5c66fb6-5b2d-57cc-b4d1-6389f608194b" "answer-f416e42b-335d-5fe1-b1a3-8afe51f7521e" "answer-6884847b-94e5-5d69-9c17-87b636deade0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e072df42-f8fc-5db8-a2ff-18aa9b23b0c3" :answer/id #uuid "e072df42-f8fc-5db8-a2ff-18aa9b23b0c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}3{t}^{2}+13\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ef8374b-3c52-5aba-a47f-1d45b49a355d" :answer/id #uuid "0ef8374b-3c52-5aba-a47f-1d45b49a355d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}5{t}^{2}+25t\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5c66fb6-5b2d-57cc-b4d1-6389f608194b" :answer/id #uuid "f5c66fb6-5b2d-57cc-b4d1-6389f608194b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}t+12\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f416e42b-335d-5fe1-b1a3-8afe51f7521e" :answer/id #uuid "f416e42b-335d-5fe1-b1a3-8afe51f7521e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{t}^{3}+20t\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6884847b-94e5-5d69-9c17-87b636deade0" :answer/id #uuid "6884847b-94e5-5d69-9c17-87b636deade0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{t}^{4}-4\\,dt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180285 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}-0]-[0-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179254 :answer-field/correct 17592186179258 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-08e87b9c-9adc-5a8c-b060-dda37adbb915" :question/id #uuid "08e87b9c-9adc-5a8c-b060-dda37adbb915" :question/math-academy-id "q-90840" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy+dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy+dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{y}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{y}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =2(\\pi +1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d5b05707-d90c-5874-90a1-dd42c5f8c325"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d5b05707-d90c-5874-90a1-dd42c5f8c325" :answer-field/id #uuid "d5b05707-d90c-5874-90a1-dd42c5f8c325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54af8bf2-64e5-57dd-8bdc-cc8a15802ee4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-54af8bf2-64e5-57dd-8bdc-cc8a15802ee4" "answer-f278dfee-2cf0-5ffd-b38d-93d20e6a0a87" "answer-a7d34679-3dab-5b88-b269-717361d9312e" "answer-7fd34733-2faf-5ece-8584-4e5eca4bc119" "answer-e00ce6fd-a2c8-597a-a47a-948b2ab26963"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54af8bf2-64e5-57dd-8bdc-cc8a15802ee4" :answer/id #uuid "54af8bf2-64e5-57dd-8bdc-cc8a15802ee4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(\\pi +1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f278dfee-2cf0-5ffd-b38d-93d20e6a0a87" :answer/id #uuid "f278dfee-2cf0-5ffd-b38d-93d20e6a0a87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(\\pi -1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7d34679-3dab-5b88-b269-717361d9312e" :answer/id #uuid "a7d34679-3dab-5b88-b269-717361d9312e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fd34733-2faf-5ece-8584-4e5eca4bc119" :answer/id #uuid "7fd34733-2faf-5ece-8584-4e5eca4bc119" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e00ce6fd-a2c8-597a-a47a-948b2ab26963" :answer/id #uuid "e00ce6fd-a2c8-597a-a47a-948b2ab26963" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b34fc554-6133-5c96-98c9-93a11d4b7862" :question/id #uuid "b34fc554-6133-5c96-98c9-93a11d4b7862" :question/math-academy-id "q-90834" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,k \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2t\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9-4t\\,dt \\\\  & =(9t-2{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(9-\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7a2b773f-820b-5bd4-95ab-10a27879c738"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7a2b773f-820b-5bd4-95ab-10a27879c738" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a2b773f-820b-5bd4-95ab-10a27879c738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ccd828c-0bdc-55ab-900f-109f767fab40" :answer-field/choices ["answer-49708f11-fe31-57c8-a150-b389b458ddef" "answer-3a8cdc89-1beb-5512-8aa9-412f93b2dc69" "answer-9ccd828c-0bdc-55ab-900f-109f767fab40" "answer-207d4a62-e83c-5373-b357-2c60fca0d97f" "answer-8110d653-b1b2-5b3d-ad4c-5d3fcbdf4a28"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49708f11-fe31-57c8-a150-b389b458ddef" :answer/id #uuid "49708f11-fe31-57c8-a150-b389b458ddef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18-\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a8cdc89-1beb-5512-8aa9-412f93b2dc69" :answer/id #uuid "3a8cdc89-1beb-5512-8aa9-412f93b2dc69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{18}(4-\\pi )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ccd828c-0bdc-55ab-900f-109f767fab40" :answer/id #uuid "9ccd828c-0bdc-55ab-900f-109f767fab40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(9-\\pi )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-207d4a62-e83c-5373-b357-2c60fca0d97f" :answer/id #uuid "207d4a62-e83c-5373-b357-2c60fca0d97f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8110d653-b1b2-5b3d-ad4c-5d3fcbdf4a28" :answer/id #uuid "8110d653-b1b2-5b3d-ad4c-5d3fcbdf4a28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7df7ee25-2f51-5474-8055-7ad2776c92c8" "question-b8cfbdaa-2d28-5714-8504-0797810897ae" "question-7e7a47fb-69a2-506b-bf8c-6f05366124e0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aa173a50-134e-5fe9-8151-0ed518869f1b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7df7ee25-2f51-5474-8055-7ad2776c92c8" :question/id #uuid "7df7ee25-2f51-5474-8055-7ad2776c92c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-20953" :question/problem "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1$ on the interval $[-2,\\frac{1}{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,0,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,\\frac{1}{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2(-2)^{3}+3(-2)^{2}+1=-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =2(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+1=2 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}+1=1 \\\\ f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(\\frac{1}{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-2)=-3,$ so the global minimum is $-3.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7741f6f67f6070d6050ebaf792598593f98a406bdab91f73356e2c27085b7a0.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4fb4d622-2cfe-5123-b12c-76f30da29551"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4fb4d622-2cfe-5123-b12c-76f30da29551" :answer-field/id #uuid "4fb4d622-2cfe-5123-b12c-76f30da29551" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-825e3c73-ea77-5644-9cd3-b6091c4271ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e7192383-05fe-5f9e-add2-9b0b63f45acd" "answer-825e3c73-ea77-5644-9cd3-b6091c4271ea" "answer-5ef16aa2-7f00-5a11-a5ae-b254c9e3ddaf" "answer-60dc99e9-55cf-552a-875b-3cbcf9519dc0" "answer-28ffe4fa-4124-53f3-9455-23c7dc4b1e77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7192383-05fe-5f9e-add2-9b0b63f45acd" :answer/id #uuid "e7192383-05fe-5f9e-add2-9b0b63f45acd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-825e3c73-ea77-5644-9cd3-b6091c4271ea" :answer/id #uuid "825e3c73-ea77-5644-9cd3-b6091c4271ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ef16aa2-7f00-5a11-a5ae-b254c9e3ddaf" :answer/id #uuid "5ef16aa2-7f00-5a11-a5ae-b254c9e3ddaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60dc99e9-55cf-552a-875b-3cbcf9519dc0" :answer/id #uuid "60dc99e9-55cf-552a-875b-3cbcf9519dc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28ffe4fa-4124-53f3-9455-23c7dc4b1e77" :answer/id #uuid "28ffe4fa-4124-53f3-9455-23c7dc4b1e77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b8cfbdaa-2d28-5714-8504-0797810897ae" :question/id #uuid "b8cfbdaa-2d28-5714-8504-0797810897ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-344375" :question/problem "The global maximum value of the function $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x+1}$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 8$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all real values of $x$ in the interval. So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=0,8$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =2\\sqrt{0+1}=2 \\\\ f(8) & =2\\sqrt{8+1}=2\\sqrt{9}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(8)=6,$ so the global maximum is $6.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\n![](images/de/def7a0e41c8534710eec8bfa3c0f6795b5fbb20ea09058403046be5bef4594fb.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b88e7600-2c1a-57b5-9965-7ce4ab955ff3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b88e7600-2c1a-57b5-9965-7ce4ab955ff3" :answer-field/id #uuid "b88e7600-2c1a-57b5-9965-7ce4ab955ff3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2c5dee35-f28d-5bb4-8fe4-b2713d716fc2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c5dee35-f28d-5bb4-8fe4-b2713d716fc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c5dee35-f28d-5bb4-8fe4-b2713d716fc2" :answer/id #uuid "2c5dee35-f28d-5bb4-8fe4-b2713d716fc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e7a47fb-69a2-506b-bf8c-6f05366124e0" :question/id #uuid "7e7a47fb-69a2-506b-bf8c-6f05366124e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344385" :question/problem "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ on the closed interval $-3\\le x\\le 5$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all real values of $x$ in the interval. So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=-3,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =\\sqrt{-3+4}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ f(5) & =\\sqrt{5+4}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(5)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-3)=1,$ so the global minimum is $1.$\n\n![](images/a6/a638683e712156f829a3d6562de44c6c72b7fb6d2c360fa293b3dbaea7297ea9.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ead7aa1-dd1c-551b-9401-863495d3e597"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ead7aa1-dd1c-551b-9401-863495d3e597" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ead7aa1-dd1c-551b-9401-863495d3e597" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a82340d7-8b4b-58b4-bb04-953eac68b378" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a82340d7-8b4b-58b4-bb04-953eac68b378"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a82340d7-8b4b-58b4-bb04-953eac68b378" :answer/id #uuid "a82340d7-8b4b-58b4-bb04-953eac68b378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa173a50-134e-5fe9-8151-0ed518869f1b" :question/id #uuid "aa173a50-134e-5fe9-8151-0ed518869f1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-2750" :question/problem "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the closed interval $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\pi ,$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pi $ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=1 \\\\ f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\pi )=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](images/7e/7e0008d35355afebc392a4bc508bd2cf2de5dc32a108f9f9c030d75a24ece076.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4da91bd3-52bf-5e6a-a679-993bbe509e49"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4da91bd3-52bf-5e6a-a679-993bbe509e49" :answer-field/id #uuid "4da91bd3-52bf-5e6a-a679-993bbe509e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bb9b8243-3292-5c0c-9ec7-4d1fa55e8d23" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9e86a30-6dd7-5faa-ac75-5295942c7076" "answer-0bd6a7c3-ca56-5d00-ad2a-bfa5230fbbcb" "answer-c476cec3-a40b-571d-9dfd-321c34be4974" "answer-5dc5c2dd-7531-574c-aa4e-5aa3dc593b78" "answer-bb9b8243-3292-5c0c-9ec7-4d1fa55e8d23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9e86a30-6dd7-5faa-ac75-5295942c7076" :answer/id #uuid "a9e86a30-6dd7-5faa-ac75-5295942c7076" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bd6a7c3-ca56-5d00-ad2a-bfa5230fbbcb" :answer/id #uuid "0bd6a7c3-ca56-5d00-ad2a-bfa5230fbbcb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c476cec3-a40b-571d-9dfd-321c34be4974" :answer/id #uuid "c476cec3-a40b-571d-9dfd-321c34be4974" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dc5c2dd-7531-574c-aa4e-5aa3dc593b78" :answer/id #uuid "5dc5c2dd-7531-574c-aa4e-5aa3dc593b78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb9b8243-3292-5c0c-9ec7-4d1fa55e8d23" :answer/id #uuid "bb9b8243-3292-5c0c-9ec7-4d1fa55e8d23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186053913 :answer-field/choices 17592186053914] [:db/retract 17592186053913 :answer-field/choices 17592186053915] [:db/retract 17592186053913 :answer-field/choices 17592186053916] [:db/retract 17592186053913 :answer-field/choices 17592186053917] [:db/retract 17592186053913 :answer-field/choices 17592186053918] {:db/id 17592186054716 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c979c575-4198-50eb-b74a-9d92d0b93552" "question-47def0cf-3ef3-5e5d-b5dc-5aca43c3b9ae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054514 :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f162b6b0c51a94c3c752f8a6ee59d4232c5bbb2fe619c7c6273f37805c281b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. What are the coordinates of the point $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta=45^\\circ$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054717 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-132171d1-1085-585d-b8f2-7700ff87a0d2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054515 :question/problem "![](images/56/56adc0846dcc0a0f6352eb4ce411f4fffdcd6f91e20524c83bd70af1bd4d948c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},$ what is the measure of the angle $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = 60^\\circ.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054718 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1ba23b8d-4b13-599f-bc74-567006cafd11" "question-01f21d28-bd9a-5f49-bee9-224def342dbf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054517 :question/problem "![](images/16/1635b5c2b1c515601e5339a0814132fca63164e7ab2b45c93ea5c2b3f5e0358b.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3},\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\sec\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta},$ we can calculate $\\sec\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\\right)} =\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt 8} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 8}{8}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054719 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-be261196-18fe-5211-8ddf-c96cd6bddf72" "question-288e585e-09b6-54a4-9e8e-a55aaefc8005"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054516 :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7},\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\tan\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}$ and $y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}, \\qquad \\sin\\theta =\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta},$ we can calculate $\\tan\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{42} }{\\sqrt{7}} = \\sqrt 6.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c979c575-4198-50eb-b74a-9d92d0b93552" :question/id #uuid "c979c575-4198-50eb-b74a-9d92d0b93552" :question/math-academy-id "q-246761" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d1cf52a210aba670c697863822f0534669127d178e8df13d4aecfe8adf01df8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Rounding to two significant figures, the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/eb/eba7dd6d571c989a14900a91eb432a216b74717046bfaa19e5fbe4de7abe6503.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta ={20}^{∘}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{20}^{∘}=0.939\\,69…≈0.94.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf419f8a-b3c5-543e-8006-abfeca431d5d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf419f8a-b3c5-543e-8006-abfeca431d5d" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf419f8a-b3c5-543e-8006-abfeca431d5d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f654ae8d-650e-57f6-9611-fb6896357adc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f654ae8d-650e-57f6-9611-fb6896357adc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f654ae8d-650e-57f6-9611-fb6896357adc" :answer/id #uuid "f654ae8d-650e-57f6-9611-fb6896357adc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.94" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-47def0cf-3ef3-5e5d-b5dc-5aca43c3b9ae" :question/id #uuid "47def0cf-3ef3-5e5d-b5dc-5aca43c3b9ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-37891" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f64637e42c8117ac66501a06470aa805ae3cf15fddf7840317394db9acb470f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{6},$ which of the following is correct?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a9/a935d38f0306fe713c997a2904267356359fdcd8f14ca5b655e0c420a5756708.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1cab1b94-4dfd-5dc6-8b3a-13e1a63ed95c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1cab1b94-4dfd-5dc6-8b3a-13e1a63ed95c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1cab1b94-4dfd-5dc6-8b3a-13e1a63ed95c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-56f29b1c-7e49-54b7-80d6-51a39d5b565e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2fcd0af-cffa-5e74-ae4d-8d682e1820e2" "answer-0ec93734-0bdb-5eb4-9807-7caf5ce894d6" "answer-56f29b1c-7e49-54b7-80d6-51a39d5b565e" "answer-27ee5dd9-b7eb-5a9e-b074-10bf5bd72fa8" "answer-9093f9a2-7f99-5977-8d45-aec38c8f9e44"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2fcd0af-cffa-5e74-ae4d-8d682e1820e2" :answer/id #uuid "d2fcd0af-cffa-5e74-ae4d-8d682e1820e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ec93734-0bdb-5eb4-9807-7caf5ce894d6" :answer/id #uuid "0ec93734-0bdb-5eb4-9807-7caf5ce894d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56f29b1c-7e49-54b7-80d