nswer-f104ac01-a346-5997-bc8f-5574e571e0fb" :answer/id #uuid "f104ac01-a346-5997-bc8f-5574e571e0fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95f4279b-1934-5ce0-b76b-a852503dc885" :answer/id #uuid "95f4279b-1934-5ce0-b76b-a852503dc885" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b2fe475-6257-5ccc-8db9-274c6fa55fca" :answer/id #uuid "5b2fe475-6257-5ccc-8db9-274c6fa55fca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a9d26f0-861d-5a0a-95be-6706bd54fd00" :answer/id #uuid "7a9d26f0-861d-5a0a-95be-6706bd54fd00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72d91a6d-fd5d-5373-9a45-1598ad4a0d34" :answer/id #uuid "72d91a6d-fd5d-5373-9a45-1598ad4a0d34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-132171d1-1085-585d-b8f2-7700ff87a0d2" :question/id #uuid "132171d1-1085-585d-b8f2-7700ff87a0d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64521" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b7a0fe3a80bfa6a0e0d82ab46a94740ce4b73007ae9d17ceb83170bce4559ff.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{6}.$ Rounded to two decimal places, what is the measure of the angle $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/eaf0e0a6434b454b58a8f53de0445bff8bae14d8bd1ec9e8e9598f0bd80323d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta $ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})=(80.405\\,93…)^{∘}≈{80.41}^{∘}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8015e10c-af0f-5b56-a09c-f0c2ba72879d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8015e10c-af0f-5b56-a09c-f0c2ba72879d" :answer-field/id #uuid "8015e10c-af0f-5b56-a09c-f0c2ba72879d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7b011649-9a23-53a3-90b7-1f25d2c60a71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83e6ddaf-77b4-5a0e-949f-91b022125de9" "answer-8f8402ff-e7d0-5cd5-a863-849e76e4cb95" "answer-7b011649-9a23-53a3-90b7-1f25d2c60a71" "answer-7a65c10e-2ee7-5335-8ef2-65ee05b05946" "answer-b159daeb-e124-57f2-997e-d4b7b1075560"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83e6ddaf-77b4-5a0e-949f-91b022125de9" :answer/id #uuid "83e6ddaf-77b4-5a0e-949f-91b022125de9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{78.45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f8402ff-e7d0-5cd5-a863-849e76e4cb95" :answer/id #uuid "8f8402ff-e7d0-5cd5-a863-849e76e4cb95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{79.24}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b011649-9a23-53a3-90b7-1f25d2c60a71" :answer/id #uuid "7b011649-9a23-53a3-90b7-1f25d2c60a71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{80.41}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a65c10e-2ee7-5335-8ef2-65ee05b05946" :answer/id #uuid "7a65c10e-2ee7-5335-8ef2-65ee05b05946" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{81.46}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b159daeb-e124-57f2-997e-d4b7b1075560" :answer/id #uuid "b159daeb-e124-57f2-997e-d4b7b1075560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{83.15}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ba23b8d-4b13-599f-bc74-567006cafd11" :question/id #uuid "1ba23b8d-4b13-599f-bc74-567006cafd11" :question/math-academy-id "q-267390" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8feb78ada73af4c97e7ce60555cf22acf5e24da38e30424c4c5c430e6456c06d.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/41/41c6badfbcafe7bafdddca30606511d2d6c3c8bdbbcd5ec2e29ff9c4c2dd310f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-98724bad-e3c1-526b-a661-1f3e637be2df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-98724bad-e3c1-526b-a661-1f3e637be2df" :answer-field/id #uuid "98724bad-e3c1-526b-a661-1f3e637be2df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-667b63c5-fe72-5337-ad38-8eb1ffe5a17b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-667b63c5-fe72-5337-ad38-8eb1ffe5a17b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-667b63c5-fe72-5337-ad38-8eb1ffe5a17b" :answer/id #uuid "667b63c5-fe72-5337-ad38-8eb1ffe5a17b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-01f21d28-bd9a-5f49-bee9-224def342dbf" :question/id #uuid "01f21d28-bd9a-5f49-bee9-224def342dbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-84641" :question/problem "![](images/11/11b00ddb6eb89e6acf79a8d5dc32e07a90850b97d8802df293e1240f6cfe444a.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cd/cd63145c6f0513fe78da148b07064eb725c2de88ded4993dabccc0da173d6583.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1872fb4f-820b-5e4c-a4ea-11325b8f3aa5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1872fb4f-820b-5e4c-a4ea-11325b8f3aa5" :answer-field/id #uuid "1872fb4f-820b-5e4c-a4ea-11325b8f3aa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-68b38f75-3590-5f6c-b34f-83c73c9fd836" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b5b1adc9-c4cd-505a-b89f-57bdded59b80" "answer-6be28734-ccd4-54e1-836e-45a57ad99216" "answer-6c50d7fa-7bb2-54b7-b910-ec04e9ed7eb5" "answer-68b38f75-3590-5f6c-b34f-83c73c9fd836" "answer-0dc2fd04-1bfc-5280-a69c-4ed2cd156d0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5b1adc9-c4cd-505a-b89f-57bdded59b80" :answer/id #uuid "b5b1adc9-c4cd-505a-b89f-57bdded59b80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6be28734-ccd4-54e1-836e-45a57ad99216" :answer/id #uuid "6be28734-ccd4-54e1-836e-45a57ad99216" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c50d7fa-7bb2-54b7-b910-ec04e9ed7eb5" :answer/id #uuid "6c50d7fa-7bb2-54b7-b910-ec04e9ed7eb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68b38f75-3590-5f6c-b34f-83c73c9fd836" :answer/id #uuid "68b38f75-3590-5f6c-b34f-83c73c9fd836" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0dc2fd04-1bfc-5280-a69c-4ed2cd156d0a" :answer/id #uuid "0dc2fd04-1bfc-5280-a69c-4ed2cd156d0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-be261196-18fe-5211-8ddf-c96cd6bddf72" :question/id #uuid "be261196-18fe-5211-8ddf-c96cd6bddf72" :question/math-academy-id "q-124387" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5b92e2126df555db5f639bc9063586b6afc3bb2896b14edadedf183ec152ac5.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{1}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/df/df5d54940a136a53c1e1c00f8cc9d71d7b064f6e0895a029da2738cf21998a56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7654d5f-ce50-5f5a-bb2d-57f8b6d0f4f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7654d5f-ce50-5f5a-bb2d-57f8b6d0f4f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7654d5f-ce50-5f5a-bb2d-57f8b6d0f4f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-53aa5f4f-ff96-5d4f-b815-838e91c9c3a5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-53aa5f4f-ff96-5d4f-b815-838e91c9c3a5" "answer-3f8a243e-b4f0-5412-8987-90fb7ae767aa" "answer-43c32df9-34ff-54be-aa25-f9bd5b25645e" "answer-a31109d2-e5c2-5154-823a-edd90f36b36c" "answer-7e14b85b-2e42-5d8b-88e9-156a24dbe1a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53aa5f4f-ff96-5d4f-b815-838e91c9c3a5" :answer/id #uuid "53aa5f4f-ff96-5d4f-b815-838e91c9c3a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f8a243e-b4f0-5412-8987-90fb7ae767aa" :answer/id #uuid "3f8a243e-b4f0-5412-8987-90fb7ae767aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43c32df9-34ff-54be-aa25-f9bd5b25645e" :answer/id #uuid "43c32df9-34ff-54be-aa25-f9bd5b25645e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a31109d2-e5c2-5154-823a-edd90f36b36c" :answer/id #uuid "a31109d2-e5c2-5154-823a-edd90f36b36c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e14b85b-2e42-5d8b-88e9-156a24dbe1a8" :answer/id #uuid "7e14b85b-2e42-5d8b-88e9-156a24dbe1a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-288e585e-09b6-54a4-9e8e-a55aaefc8005" :question/id #uuid "288e585e-09b6-54a4-9e8e-a55aaefc8005" :question/math-academy-id "q-64981" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbff0c974c790ec4287be1dcff8aa62d0da0aa5e77fa5ec577633f5f2cad6a5d.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/1f/1ffb0a83abf7e736c47035f4f337c36a6bb129fd0d64fe0ac3bfe8e46c194a11.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd0b2e2a-2447-58b4-9753-a6af8b018534"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd0b2e2a-2447-58b4-9753-a6af8b018534" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd0b2e2a-2447-58b4-9753-a6af8b018534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b9c28105-c9bd-57cf-88ff-3460736a6d61" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50518fe9-0b2d-5dcc-89cd-eefab837bf7d" "answer-f980b97a-e2c0-55f1-af77-7659b8749bf0" "answer-ff578584-d99e-5654-8c41-d355a3df44de" "answer-5091e3af-a0e1-5296-ba59-d8a3f4c4675f" "answer-b9c28105-c9bd-57cf-88ff-3460736a6d61"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50518fe9-0b2d-5dcc-89cd-eefab837bf7d" :answer/id #uuid "50518fe9-0b2d-5dcc-89cd-eefab837bf7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f980b97a-e2c0-55f1-af77-7659b8749bf0" :answer/id #uuid "f980b97a-e2c0-55f1-af77-7659b8749bf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff578584-d99e-5654-8c41-d355a3df44de" :answer/id #uuid "ff578584-d99e-5654-8c41-d355a3df44de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5091e3af-a0e1-5296-ba59-d8a3f4c4675f" :answer/id #uuid "5091e3af-a0e1-5296-ba59-d8a3f4c4675f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9c28105-c9bd-57cf-88ff-3460736a6d61" :answer/id #uuid "b9c28105-c9bd-57cf-88ff-3460736a6d61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055959 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in the first quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/bc/bca60509a15e4d32d06d96c7cf5eac65a00b4e5cb72242152dd998b933209d7d.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $P$ coincides with the cosine of the angle $\\theta,$ and\n\n- the $y$-coordinate coincides with the sine of the angle $\\theta.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nConsequently, if we know the angle $\\theta,$ we can easily work out the coordinates of the point $P,$ and vice-versa. This works regardless of whether the angle $\\theta$ is given in degrees or radians.\n\nFor now, we'll focus on points located in the first quadrant. Later, we will encounter points in all quadrants." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055960 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the unit circle and a point $P(x,y)$ that lies on the circle.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nTo prove that $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta,$ let's examine the triangle that's formed by $\\overline{OP}$ and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/53/53d84bedcddcb484bc613a2f01e002afe16b614daa909ff744195bcc45464472.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that: \n\n- The hypotenuse has length $1$ since it is a radius of the unit circle.\n\n- The adjacent and opposite sides have lengths $x$ and $y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, using the definitions of $\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{1} = x, \\qquad  \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{1} = y.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055963 :tutorial/content "To find the tangent of an angle using the unit circle, we need to use the relationship\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let's take a look at the following triangle.\n\n![](images/99/99b51d7d25a266dbfda7839be6bafc335b181ecd6dc74224fcb0557180f872aa.png)\n\nFor this particular triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{z}, \\qquad \\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{z}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nWhen we take $\\sin\\theta$ and divide it by $\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{y}{z}÷\\frac{x}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{z}\\cdot \\frac{z}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot z}{x\\cdot z} \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot \\cancel{z}}{x\\cdot \\cancel{z}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough this proof is only for acute angles, the identity is true for any angle, provided that $\\tan\\theta$ is well-defined.\n\nFinally, since $\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta},$ it immediately follows that\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054870 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068879 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f49a62d5-679a-524a-aa83-c1365bd71520" "question-6bcd8b40-daaf-5baf-a297-367e7c76cde5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f49a62d5-679a-524a-aa83-c1365bd71520" :question/id #uuid "f49a62d5-679a-524a-aa83-c1365bd71520" :question/math-academy-id "q-12786" :question/problem "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $p$ is $4{p}^{2}=25.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{p}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 4{p}^{2}-25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{p}^{2}-25 & =0 \\\\ (2p)^{2}-{5}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (2p-5)(2p+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n2p-5=0\\,\\text{or}\\,2p+5=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-5 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 2p+5 & =0 \\\\ 2p & =5 & \\, & \\, & 2p & =-5 \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{2} & \\, & \\, & p & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5}{2}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $p=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c9e8053f-1d19-507b-ae24-2f03876f5694"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c9e8053f-1d19-507b-ae24-2f03876f5694" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9e8053f-1d19-507b-ae24-2f03876f5694" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8de39a43-8ede-5187-ba08-b187507759ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d8327b6-7b01-5a19-936b-ca09e8613a7d" "answer-7d1a02f4-ee95-57b6-97e6-2580b8e0c2e6" "answer-d6d3906e-8813-5496-bc23-8f6231b7e268" "answer-ee107525-6275-5cbd-ab75-f56bf4255a19" "answer-8de39a43-8ede-5187-ba08-b187507759ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d8327b6-7b01-5a19-936b-ca09e8613a7d" :answer/id #uuid "3d8327b6-7b01-5a19-936b-ca09e8613a7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d1a02f4-ee95-57b6-97e6-2580b8e0c2e6" :answer/id #uuid "7d1a02f4-ee95-57b6-97e6-2580b8e0c2e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=0,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6d3906e-8813-5496-bc23-8f6231b7e268" :answer/id #uuid "d6d3906e-8813-5496-bc23-8f6231b7e268" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=2,5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee107525-6275-5cbd-ab75-f56bf4255a19" :answer/id #uuid "ee107525-6275-5cbd-ab75-f56bf4255a19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=\\pm \\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8de39a43-8ede-5187-ba08-b187507759ab" :answer/id #uuid "8de39a43-8ede-5187-ba08-b187507759ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6bcd8b40-daaf-5baf-a297-367e7c76cde5" :question/id #uuid "6bcd8b40-daaf-5baf-a297-367e7c76cde5" :question/math-academy-id "q-44216" :question/problem "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $100{x}^{2}=7.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2} & =7 \\\\ 100{x}^{2}-7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2}-7 & =0 \\\\ (10x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (10x-\\sqrt{7})(10x+\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n10x-\\sqrt{7}=0\\,\\text{or}\\,10x+\\sqrt{7}=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\sqrt{7} & =0 & \\, & \\, & 10x+\\sqrt{7} & =0 \\\\ 10x & =\\sqrt{7} & \\, & \\, & 10x & =-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10} & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab8d9acb-6fc3-559a-a240-a008f18351b1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab8d9acb-6fc3-559a-a240-a008f18351b1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8d9acb-6fc3-559a-a240-a008f18351b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bcde4af1-a530-531b-be8b-3a0aa122b238" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ccd2a13c-9442-5d35-a48b-3bbad053d7bf" "answer-8ec6ff9a-236c-5222-9a36-a98ddc2fec27" "answer-7363154c-84f1-50eb-a664-4c273bbb58c9" "answer-bcde4af1-a530-531b-be8b-3a0aa122b238" "answer-b911aa7c-d630-5d40-a422-58c6b53d97f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccd2a13c-9442-5d35-a48b-3bbad053d7bf" :answer/id #uuid "ccd2a13c-9442-5d35-a48b-3bbad053d7bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{10}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ec6ff9a-236c-5222-9a36-a98ddc2fec27" :answer/id #uuid "8ec6ff9a-236c-5222-9a36-a98ddc2fec27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{7}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7363154c-84f1-50eb-a664-4c273bbb58c9" :answer/id #uuid "7363154c-84f1-50eb-a664-4c273bbb58c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcde4af1-a530-531b-be8b-3a0aa122b238" :answer/id #uuid "bcde4af1-a530-531b-be8b-3a0aa122b238" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b911aa7c-d630-5d40-a422-58c6b53d97f9" :answer/id #uuid "b911aa7c-d630-5d40-a422-58c6b53d97f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068878 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-36228bc3-c8df-58ee-9fb1-b8814e29ddd8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-36228bc3-c8df-58ee-9fb1-b8814e29ddd8" :question/id #uuid "36228bc3-c8df-58ee-9fb1-b8814e29ddd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-111997" :question/problem "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $16{y}^{2}-81=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{y}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ (4y)^{2}-(9)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (4y-9)(4y+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n4y-9=0\\,\\text{or}\\,4y+9=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-9 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 4y+9 & =0 \\\\ 4y & =9 & \\, & \\, & 4y & =-9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{4} & \\, & \\, & y & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{9}{4},\\frac{9}{4}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $y=\\pm \\frac{9}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ced7671a-bc8b-5661-8f1a-fdc445d654be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ced7671a-bc8b-5661-8f1a-fdc445d654be" :answer-field/id #uuid "ced7671a-bc8b-5661-8f1a-fdc445d654be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34c8611d-f4d0-5fc3-bc5b-7bb9b1dcfd80" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6810cf2e-dde9-5c88-ba30-9bf1db5e3988" "answer-d7fe1427-aadb-56ed-9930-60e09e59d9b0" "answer-c793206a-9ed2-5ff6-90f1-25919555ed3a" "answer-7e5080fb-4356-5e0d-810b-29ca0123aff1" "answer-34c8611d-f4d0-5fc3-bc5b-7bb9b1dcfd80"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6810cf2e-dde9-5c88-ba30-9bf1db5e3988" :answer/id #uuid "6810cf2e-dde9-5c88-ba30-9bf1db5e3988" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7fe1427-aadb-56ed-9930-60e09e59d9b0" :answer/id #uuid "d7fe1427-aadb-56ed-9930-60e09e59d9b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{81}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c793206a-9ed2-5ff6-90f1-25919555ed3a" :answer/id #uuid "c793206a-9ed2-5ff6-90f1-25919555ed3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{81}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e5080fb-4356-5e0d-810b-29ca0123aff1" :answer/id #uuid "7e5080fb-4356-5e0d-810b-29ca0123aff1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34c8611d-f4d0-5fc3-bc5b-7bb9b1dcfd80" :answer/id #uuid "34c8611d-f4d0-5fc3-bc5b-7bb9b1dcfd80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-cc3bb6b8-928e-5335-a818-2702f23810ea" :activity/id #uuid "cc3bb6b8-928e-5335-a818-2702f23810ea" :activity/title "Solving Quadratic Equations Using a Difference of Squares" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048157 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-d43a116c-df02-565c-9c0d-c8994337e176" "step-314b4754-6b4d-5560-bc0e-1a66b0c11feb" "step-df79d613-c2bb-5858-bb0e-95831b62fe81"] :activity/first-step "step-d43a116c-df02-565c-9c0d-c8994337e176" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d43a116c-df02-565c-9c0d-c8994337e176" :step/id #uuid "d43a116c-df02-565c-9c0d-c8994337e176" :step/content "question-f49a62d5-679a-524a-aa83-c1365bd71520" :step/next "step-314b4754-6b4d-5560-bc0e-1a66b0c11feb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-314b4754-6b4d-5560-bc0e-1a66b0c11feb" :step/id #uuid "314b4754-6b4d-5560-bc0e-1a66b0c11feb" :step/content "question-6bcd8b40-daaf-5baf-a297-367e7c76cde5" :step/next "step-df79d613-c2bb-5858-bb0e-95831b62fe81" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-df79d613-c2bb-5858-bb0e-95831b62fe81" :step/id #uuid "df79d613-c2bb-5858-bb0e-95831b62fe81" :step/content "question-36228bc3-c8df-58ee-9fb1-b8814e29ddd8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186102264 :answer-field/choices 17592186102265] [:db/retract 17592186102264 :answer-field/choices 17592186102266] [:db/retract 17592186102264 :answer-field/choices 17592186102267] [:db/retract 17592186102264 :answer-field/choices 17592186102268] [:db/retract 17592186102264 :answer-field/choices 17592186102269] [:db/retract 17592186102258 :answer-field/choices 17592186102259] [:db/retract 17592186102258 :answer-field/choices 17592186102260] [:db/retract 17592186102258 :answer-field/choices 17592186102262] [:db/retract 17592186102258 :answer-field/choices 17592186102263] {:db/id 17592186105889 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f409435d-6032-5802-bf1f-c20613731f7b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105887 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e16a799c-84bb-5d3d-b669-6d70bdf08db4" "question-9f471502-6ac3-5895-874a-d7c88c5897e2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105888 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1bae08c0-8385-58de-b3f9-2f81e17da99b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f409435d-6032-5802-bf1f-c20613731f7b" :question/id #uuid "f409435d-6032-5802-bf1f-c20613731f7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161327" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(1)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2(x+\\sqrt{2}y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1808b286-0193-5bc8-a2c6-690d66c8a263"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1808b286-0193-5bc8-a2c6-690d66c8a263" :answer-field/id #uuid "1808b286-0193-5bc8-a2c6-690d66c8a263" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9877fc34-f311-559c-8cc0-f65810b60958" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1ff7c33-ae77-5848-9080-a0accbff86b7" "answer-9877fc34-f311-559c-8cc0-f65810b60958" "answer-ea232514-d5a5-5e3b-af1e-b89e96005805" "answer-228ec946-8873-5f8b-8a2a-49f36370152e" "answer-c20f1d54-0fa6-57df-9370-78329241cb08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1ff7c33-ae77-5848-9080-a0accbff86b7" :answer/id #uuid "a1ff7c33-ae77-5848-9080-a0accbff86b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2({x}^{2}+2y+2\\sqrt{2}xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9877fc34-f311-559c-8cc0-f65810b60958" :answer/id #uuid "9877fc34-f311-559c-8cc0-f65810b60958" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea232514-d5a5-5e3b-af1e-b89e96005805" :answer/id #uuid "ea232514-d5a5-5e3b-af1e-b89e96005805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-228ec946-8873-5f8b-8a2a-49f36370152e" :answer/id #uuid "228ec946-8873-5f8b-8a2a-49f36370152e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2({x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c20f1d54-0fa6-57df-9370-78329241cb08" :answer/id #uuid "c20f1d54-0fa6-57df-9370-78329241cb08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2({x}^{2}y+4{y}^{2}+\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106063 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=-3xz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,-3x(\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,-3\\sqrt{10}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102264 :answer-field/correct "answer-c85fc074-c3f3-5f8a-adf8-d4d09b35c0c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3bacac8-fa44-5629-8e2e-433fe9d6399b" "answer-b1ba54ed-3698-5620-9d88-6495964971dc" "answer-c85fc074-c3f3-5f8a-adf8-d4d09b35c0c2" "answer-71cc19bf-1ed6-5dcd-844c-039bf3f357ea" "answer-1b51fb05-18e5-5810-881e-a944e482d7d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3bacac8-fa44-5629-8e2e-433fe9d6399b" :answer/id #uuid "c3bacac8-fa44-5629-8e2e-433fe9d6399b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10\\sqrt{3}({x}^{2}-xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1ba54ed-3698-5620-9d88-6495964971dc" :answer/id #uuid "b1ba54ed-3698-5620-9d88-6495964971dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{10}(-{x}^{2}y+xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c85fc074-c3f3-5f8a-adf8-d4d09b35c0c2" :answer/id #uuid "c85fc074-c3f3-5f8a-adf8-d4d09b35c0c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{10}({x}^{2}+xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71cc19bf-1ed6-5dcd-844c-039bf3f357ea" :answer/id #uuid "71cc19bf-1ed6-5dcd-844c-039bf3f357ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{5x}({x}^{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b51fb05-18e5-5810-881e-a944e482d7d8" :answer/id #uuid "1b51fb05-18e5-5810-881e-a944e482d7d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}+2xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e16a799c-84bb-5d3d-b669-6d70bdf08db4" :question/id #uuid "e16a799c-84bb-5d3d-b669-6d70bdf08db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161350" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-3x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $3x\\le y\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-3x)=-3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-3x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{1+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{3x}^{3}\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}(y-3x)^{3/2}]}_{y=3x}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-3x)^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\,{[\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})(3-3x)^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(0-{3}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d007d0a-e619-522c-9617-4f4a41fec80b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d007d0a-e619-522c-9617-4f4a41fec80b" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d007d0a-e619-522c-9617-4f4a41fec80b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2aa47af8-9c69-50d1-901d-f11e4c5ee62b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3809e723-7c0b-5a79-a57f-42ba07e98577" "answer-1f64d453-abc2-52b8-9d32-f5007c10820a" "answer-2aa47af8-9c69-50d1-901d-f11e4c5ee62b" "answer-90b9897e-bd16-5166-a704-48996320daaf" "answer-22931441-e80d-5d69-b5da-e45afb70211d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3809e723-7c0b-5a79-a57f-42ba07e98577" :answer/id #uuid "3809e723-7c0b-5a79-a57f-42ba07e98577" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f64d453-abc2-52b8-9d32-f5007c10820a" :answer/id #uuid "1f64d453-abc2-52b8-9d32-f5007c10820a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{5}(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2aa47af8-9c69-50d1-901d-f11e4c5ee62b" :answer/id #uuid "2aa47af8-9c69-50d1-901d-f11e4c5ee62b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90b9897e-bd16-5166-a704-48996320daaf" :answer/id #uuid "90b9897e-bd16-5166-a704-48996320daaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}(2-\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22931441-e80d-5d69-b5da-e45afb70211d" :answer/id #uuid "22931441-e80d-5d69-b5da-e45afb70211d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106057 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}12xyz\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}12xyz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,12xy(x+y)\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,12xy(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,12{x}^{2}y+12x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{x}^{2}y+12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\,[2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(2+2)-0 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102228 :answer-field/correct 17592186102231 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9f471502-6ac3-5895-874a-d7c88c5897e2" :question/id #uuid "9f471502-6ac3-5895-874a-d7c88c5897e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161354" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $x\\le y\\le x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}+{0}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{x+1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}y]}_{y=x}^{y=x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}(1+{e}^{2x})^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-de37fba6-41ca-540b-a8fd-47ec845611d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-de37fba6-41ca-540b-a8fd-47ec845611d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "de37fba6-41ca-540b-a8fd-47ec845611d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ce0a3efb-4ed4-5af7-8085-fc0e737a543c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d59e1bf3-fe94-540a-b645-1d3ca9e31091" "answer-8726e024-292d-59b4-8013-fd7bf0f67e0d" "answer-ce0a3efb-4ed4-5af7-8085-fc0e737a543c" "answer-c5558c16-5207-5627-b751-3fcb910575e2" "answer-c66cb474-bc45-59be-8448-dccc9423f5ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d59e1bf3-fe94-540a-b645-1d3ca9e31091" :answer/id #uuid "d59e1bf3-fe94-540a-b645-1d3ca9e31091" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+e)^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8726e024-292d-59b4-8013-fd7bf0f67e0d" :answer/id #uuid "8726e024-292d-59b4-8013-fd7bf0f67e0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+e)^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce0a3efb-4ed4-5af7-8085-fc0e737a543c" :answer/id #uuid "ce0a3efb-4ed4-5af7-8085-fc0e737a543c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5558c16-5207-5627-b751-3fcb910575e2" :answer/id #uuid "c5558c16-5207-5627-b751-3fcb910575e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((2+e)^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c66cb474-bc45-59be-8448-dccc9423f5ad" :answer/id #uuid "c66cb474-bc45-59be-8448-dccc9423f5ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((2+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278480 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186415983 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}(1-{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1bae08c0-8385-58de-b3f9-2f81e17da99b" :question/id #uuid "1bae08c0-8385-58de-b3f9-2f81e17da99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161328" :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a911c19c-a198-57f8-8ad5-1e251d33cc93"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a911c19c-a198-57f8-8ad5-1e251d33cc93" :answer-field/id #uuid "a911c19c-a198-57f8-8ad5-1e251d33cc93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5efef0b8-d72d-5a49-aec0-94be999fd7a8" "answer-54ae8c91-054f-56c1-8736-87af40558e09" "answer-9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" "answer-c7c386ae-4bfc-514a-a465-86fbebbe089e" "answer-4933e24f-9535-56e6-8bd8-463ca459b1f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5efef0b8-d72d-5a49-aec0-94be999fd7a8" :answer/id #uuid "5efef0b8-d72d-5a49-aec0-94be999fd7a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54ae8c91-054f-56c1-8736-87af40558e09" :answer/id #uuid "54ae8c91-054f-56c1-8736-87af40558e09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer/id #uuid "9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7c386ae-4bfc-514a-a465-86fbebbe089e" :answer/id #uuid "c7c386ae-4bfc-514a-a465-86fbebbe089e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4933e24f-9535-56e6-8bd8-463ca459b1f4" :answer/id #uuid "4933e24f-9535-56e6-8bd8-463ca459b1f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106062 :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x-y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102258 :answer-field/correct "answer-887450bf-a003-50a9-abb2-58774fef157d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70656859-96a1-5e8a-8599-832418c1e00a" "answer-887450bf-a003-50a9-abb2-58774fef157d" "answer-d4b4642d-b2a3-5966-82c9-11412eda5001" "answer-8dea7678-d219-511f-b3fe-1db777950957"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70656859-96a1-5e8a-8599-832418c1e00a" :answer/id #uuid "70656859-96a1-5e8a-8599-832418c1e00a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-887450bf-a003-50a9-abb2-58774fef157d" :answer/id #uuid "887450bf-a003-50a9-abb2-58774fef157d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\sqrt{6}\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4b4642d-b2a3-5966-82c9-11412eda5001" :answer/id #uuid "d4b4642d-b2a3-5966-82c9-11412eda5001" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dea7678-d219-511f-b3fe-1db777950957" :answer/id #uuid "8dea7678-d219-511f-b3fe-1db777950957" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278469 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32(1+1) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115301 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e80b3506-231f-529b-b14d-6bb0a866165f" "question-a85d5623-8f4e-5539-b87a-051d9e911395" "question-31757f7a-27f2-5cad-90a3-e9168497ad54" "question-236b0649-a813-5e30-b589-a9199c88af15" "question-5f36b991-1cba-5c10-9333-13a2ade76e0a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115126 :question/problem "![](images/40/401368d830825a55e27fc6ba191a1c3dbb80d93b2e0af74db3337523817d2c65.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2,$ for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1,$ as shown above. Find an integral that represents the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=1$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/04/0434e16502f9b20bb53f3c46ba265239c59e8f5a487cdf3e71962fc0019a927d.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115280 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f0334985-4e7c-56a4-ba8b-035c69ca7733" "question-4017e457-d6e9-5799-a313-b509dcbd530f" "question-6b9b18aa-2c78-5504-8e8f-08d14250da87" "question-48038fbf-e0e6-55ac-98d1-e8c48a8b376a" "question-3fd041ce-cfe7-560a-8362-3b96b6047662"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115121 :question/problem "![](images/70/70dc1594c0072b83429fc1f5d6be4fa454f6650c3eeef37c41c2d3ec691bb35f.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=x^2$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ from $x=0$ to $x=1,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $360^{\\circ}$about the line $y=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b0427e7d37245d86d347d6d4f867ae3d17e5f0a030feb53d28ad4bbc856f85c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tV &= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\big[\\: \\left({\\color{blue}\\textrm{upper function}}\\right) - \\left({\\color{brown}\\textrm{lower function}}\\right) \\:\\big]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t \t&= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\left[\\: {\\color{blue}x^2 }- \\left({\\color{brown}-1} \\right)\\:\\right]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]  \n                &= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+1)^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^4+2x^2+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{x^5}{5}+\\dfrac{2x^3}{3}+x \\right) \\Bigg|_{0}^{1} \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{2}{3}+1 \\right] - 0 \\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{28 \\pi}{15}.\n}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115281 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-059b810f-7faa-5870-a600-8d8d6445cd02" "question-4fe06b9c-2dd8-5751-a283-a97db5419aa5" "question-b4260f61-3124-52b4-9558-94f62f160010" "question-27279421-a932-5375-bd1a-4824ccac1d67" "question-a978de85-c2b4-579a-92f4-5064fe99c547"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115122 :question/problem "![](images/e9/e99f6cd10a72c91e5c7430a9ee18a45ba4dc09f941c779077567436a5fe90405.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x-1},$ the vertical line $x=1,$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=1,$ as shown in the picture. Find an integral expression for the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=y^3+1\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/73/73fc04f5a8a56b0faba6ec2a77f8aafd864c84786adfe7da9ccda99d0c8a4d8f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=y^3+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,({y}^{3}+1)-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{({y}^{3})}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{6}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115302 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0ccddc8e-6343-5b87-aa99-4a041586cf69" "question-08dd2009-a210-5f29-bbaa-81488a5664f6" "question-2588c644-3085-550f-abbf-182631d5c4e8" "question-6ffaec2e-3741-5b7e-88f5-bdff517c9f5d" "question-2f10ec35-272f-563f-8af5-2c0fc2e74c5d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115127 :question/problem "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt{y},$ the vertical line $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=4,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=2.$\n\n![](images/a5/a56f30e5912629c9854e68d4837a37dae55a005e98ef6cf5379b5eb81022afb7.png)" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. To do so, we find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =2 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=4$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/07/0748c87aa004f06f99a19ab4b7a2dadcf2f86920b53d1edd7ff19aced1d76f04.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(2)-(\\sqrt{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(4-4\\sqrt{y}+y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {[4y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3/2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi [(4(4)-\\frac{8}{3}(4)^{3/2}+\\frac{(4)^{2}}{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =\\pi [16-\\frac{64}{3}+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e80b3506-231f-529b-b14d-6bb0a866165f" :question/id #uuid "e80b3506-231f-529b-b14d-6bb0a866165f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49890" :question/problem "![](images/28/2817662752008c41b76d1beb9bec73907f55d836524fe48c583997670421f70b.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2},$ for $x\\in [-2,\\frac{5}{2}],$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e1/e1774466f020515d3296f2e48fda0a61154bddbfd2285a67c97bdc7e35b60da5.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(2{x}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-36e290c7-ae9f-5409-9b4f-541a37e1ca78"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-36e290c7-ae9f-5409-9b4f-541a37e1ca78" :answer-field/id #uuid "36e290c7-ae9f-5409-9b4f-541a37e1ca78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8607c785-004e-5380-8266-895cff651e5a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5cc19dac-699d-59b0-87b6-ff3f7c64d409" "answer-be6e9ea7-3b03-5b53-ba44-976064339a53" "answer-85da4021-495f-522c-b62f-28b0804dabf1" "answer-8607c785-004e-5380-8266-895cff651e5a" "answer-6ad741f4-7070-5f9a-8539-bd9c8f502b2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cc19dac-699d-59b0-87b6-ff3f7c64d409" :answer/id #uuid "5cc19dac-699d-59b0-87b6-ff3f7c64d409" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{25/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be6e9ea7-3b03-5b53-ba44-976064339a53" :answer/id #uuid "be6e9ea7-3b03-5b53-ba44-976064339a53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}(4{x}^{4}+4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85da4021-495f-522c-b62f-28b0804dabf1" :answer/id #uuid "85da4021-495f-522c-b62f-28b0804dabf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(2{x}^{2}+4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8607c785-004e-5380-8266-895cff651e5a" :answer/id #uuid "8607c785-004e-5380-8266-895cff651e5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ad741f4-7070-5f9a-8539-bd9c8f502b2c" :answer/id #uuid "6ad741f4-7070-5f9a-8539-bd9c8f502b2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a85d5623-8f4e-5539-b87a-051d9e911395" :question/id #uuid "a85d5623-8f4e-5539-b87a-051d9e911395" :question/math-academy-id "q-50020" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d27538da9cf9ebb39324477a9c3e9eabd950b648507ebc616a11d5c8a6054a19.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ for $x\\in [0,2],$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/94/94d3f7e76db23c387366024bdd5dbb25f8adddc25d78bb3fc81f5900b4128fcf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y={x}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,({x}^{3})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{6}+2{x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e8b2b3c4-71d4-527c-abf3-f3422fc41129"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8b2b3c4-71d4-527c-abf3-f3422fc41129" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b2b3c4-71d4-527c-abf3-f3422fc41129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ccdea9f5-56f5-5aa5-9f72-703a22f500d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-13e479de-ced4-5c94-a17a-d38d70a5d8f9" "answer-e855cc0b-f431-5b79-a5c7-f010271a1ca2" "answer-ab2bf703-4e49-50bf-9ab2-b775bfc98742" "answer-ccdea9f5-56f5-5aa5-9f72-703a22f500d1" "answer-45a333a3-e039-5463-812c-9bff893827ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13e479de-ced4-5c94-a17a-d38d70a5d8f9" :answer/id #uuid "13e479de-ced4-5c94-a17a-d38d70a5d8f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2{x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e855cc0b-f431-5b79-a5c7-f010271a1ca2" :answer/id #uuid "e855cc0b-f431-5b79-a5c7-f010271a1ca2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{6}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab2bf703-4e49-50bf-9ab2-b775bfc98742" :answer/id #uuid "ab2bf703-4e49-50bf-9ab2-b775bfc98742" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{6}+2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccdea9f5-56f5-5aa5-9f72-703a22f500d1" :answer/id #uuid "ccdea9f5-56f5-5aa5-9f72-703a22f500d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{6}+2{x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45a333a3-e039-5463-812c-9bff893827ab" :answer/id #uuid "45a333a3-e039-5463-812c-9bff893827ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{0}(4x-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-31757f7a-27f2-5cad-90a3-e9168497ad54" :question/id #uuid "31757f7a-27f2-5cad-90a3-e9168497ad54" :question/math-academy-id "q-120535" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fa5e5b2300c3395d48f5355352cd6ece7477f3bae8e8c185f0f869fa197b0e9.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x$ and the horizontal line $y=2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2)+(x-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/5a/5a1821ff500c901d9f072fe56101f17e579018d0710543c4a2e371214894725c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0cfa96a4-e989-5e97-a3e8-83dc73708ba8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0cfa96a4-e989-5e97-a3e8-83dc73708ba8" :answer-field/id #uuid "0cfa96a4-e989-5e97-a3e8-83dc73708ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f95b558e-ab41-5c5f-b335-0ae58978c14b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9811b230-7ef8-5ebc-8a3f-b70ca6db6be5" "answer-2d517681-8ca3-5922-a210-f7d68fbd36ad" "answer-8f568eb4-6ce0-559e-81d7-859c5216f036" "answer-f95b558e-ab41-5c5f-b335-0ae58978c14b" "answer-6fb3753a-fee0-5ce8-b33a-4e43e42c5870"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9811b230-7ef8-5ebc-8a3f-b70ca6db6be5" :answer/id #uuid "9811b230-7ef8-5ebc-8a3f-b70ca6db6be5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d517681-8ca3-5922-a210-f7d68fbd36ad" :answer/id #uuid "2d517681-8ca3-5922-a210-f7d68fbd36ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f568eb4-6ce0-559e-81d7-859c5216f036" :answer/id #uuid "8f568eb4-6ce0-559e-81d7-859c5216f036" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f95b558e-ab41-5c5f-b335-0ae58978c14b" :answer/id #uuid "f95b558e-ab41-5c5f-b335-0ae58978c14b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fb3753a-fee0-5ce8-b33a-4e43e42c5870" :answer/id #uuid "6fb3753a-fee0-5ce8-b33a-4e43e42c5870" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-236b0649-a813-5e30-b589-a9199c88af15" :question/id #uuid "236b0649-a813-5e30-b589-a9199c88af15" :question/math-academy-id "q-50022" :question/problem "![](images/10/1066e10a5a9860e371ca2895ec39f7cdce4e000d5cca9f3b478f57cd890d647a.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ for $x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ and the horizontal line $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4c086c9a3a99609728bc7c53f5a28ac86431e5c37e2b87bcfd3031a32aa470.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{1}{x})-(\\frac{1}{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-01397a07-f9d3-51fc-adc8-145fbda696db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-01397a07-f9d3-51fc-adc8-145fbda696db" :answer-field/id #uuid "01397a07-f9d3-51fc-adc8-145fbda696db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-887bb682-847d-5497-b082-069e31b4ca18" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa69c2e1-351a-5624-8415-3234bb23b87e" "answer-9e084eaf-fd07-5c31-9a44-9d16ecb72243" "answer-887bb682-847d-5497-b082-069e31b4ca18" "answer-8f35e7c9-23db-5d10-9546-2d16da17d83e" "answer-eeaa6543-a7cf-5a69-8ac8-dcbdbdc1fd1a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa69c2e1-351a-5624-8415-3234bb23b87e" :answer/id #uuid "aa69c2e1-351a-5624-8415-3234bb23b87e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e084eaf-fd07-5c31-9a44-9d16ecb72243" :answer/id #uuid "9e084eaf-fd07-5c31-9a44-9d16ecb72243" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-887bb682-847d-5497-b082-069e31b4ca18" :answer/id #uuid "887bb682-847d-5497-b082-069e31b4ca18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f35e7c9-23db-5d10-9546-2d16da17d83e" :answer/id #uuid "8f35e7c9-23db-5d10-9546-2d16da17d83e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeaa6543-a7cf-5a69-8ac8-dcbdbdc1fd1a" :answer/id #uuid "eeaa6543-a7cf-5a69-8ac8-dcbdbdc1fd1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f36b991-1cba-5c10-9333-13a2ade76e0a" :question/id #uuid "5f36b991-1cba-5c10-9333-13a2ade76e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50007" :question/problem "![](images/84/848b73762d79b0b7b898f3c011e4118256b5fca37fa9d702313bc16da04b4c3d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2},$ for $x\\in [0,1],$ and the horizontal line $y=2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b634be23e06f6038e00b91f27eadacd1d1bcc51516d741786cc433a1ce731ce.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{x}^{4}-8{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-10a2ecc1-4bf8-5461-93f0-d493b4fcede2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-10a2ecc1-4bf8-5461-93f0-d493b4fcede2" :answer-field/id #uuid "10a2ecc1-4bf8-5461-93f0-d493b4fcede2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e3b82ef7-2f85-5c83-94fb-8edac9a6b879" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc8852ed-05e5-5668-baa9-257a358a079a" "answer-0e395b02-9179-5af5-8f19-5bbe78d139f0" "answer-e8576b89-9011-5d9b-86c1-433e60fde2d7" "answer-bbb763f1-9aef-52ca-9fd8-c473b0438b5b" "answer-e3b82ef7-2f85-5c83-94fb-8edac9a6b879"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc8852ed-05e5-5668-baa9-257a358a079a" :answer/id #uuid "fc8852ed-05e5-5668-baa9-257a358a079a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e395b02-9179-5af5-8f19-5bbe78d139f0" :answer/id #uuid "0e395b02-9179-5af5-8f19-5bbe78d139f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8576b89-9011-5d9b-86c1-433e60fde2d7" :answer/id #uuid "e8576b89-9011-5d9b-86c1-433e60fde2d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbb763f1-9aef-52ca-9fd8-c473b0438b5b" :answer/id #uuid "bbb763f1-9aef-52ca-9fd8-c473b0438b5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3b82ef7-2f85-5c83-94fb-8edac9a6b879" :answer/id #uuid "e3b82ef7-2f85-5c83-94fb-8edac9a6b879" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0334985-4e7c-56a4-ba8b-035c69ca7733" :question/id #uuid "f0334985-4e7c-56a4-ba8b-035c69ca7733" :question/math-academy-id "q-81681" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c21d4794aeedcc70fe037a936724d2708a484d43c457b2d120927f86f1fa6ffc.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x-2$ and the horizontal line $y=-2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-2 & =-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e7/e785138362c91f9034d90ee9d923fc818cd299b3ee5d6d25eb1bf40cabc2f2b0.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-2x-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(-2)-({x}^{2}-2x-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2x)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([\\frac{1}{5}(2)^{5}-(2)^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}(2)^{3}\\text{ }]-[0]) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{32}{5}-16+\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b052f20-a657-5fb9-97d6-3e43da2ac8be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b052f20-a657-5fb9-97d6-3e43da2ac8be" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b052f20-a657-5fb9-97d6-3e43da2ac8be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-87805274-7994-5b8b-82ff-5ddcf34f2cd9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7380ba8b-27af-582b-adbb-5535ef77e4f8" "answer-87805274-7994-5b8b-82ff-5ddcf34f2cd9" "answer-79b24dfe-6073-564c-9ec8-d4c228f54ebe" "answer-fdb25f6e-a8f3-5f13-96fb-d6e19cd7bc06" "answer-d61da461-e738-5414-9b19-a2d59208047c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7380ba8b-27af-582b-adbb-5535ef77e4f8" :answer/id #uuid "7380ba8b-27af-582b-adbb-5535ef77e4f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14\\pi }{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87805274-7994-5b8b-82ff-5ddcf34f2cd9" :answer/id #uuid "87805274-7994-5b8b-82ff-5ddcf34f2cd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79b24dfe-6073-564c-9ec8-d4c228f54ebe" :answer/id #uuid "79b24dfe-6073-564c-9ec8-d4c228f54ebe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdb25f6e-a8f3-5f13-96fb-d6e19cd7bc06" :answer/id #uuid "fdb25f6e-a8f3-5f13-96fb-d6e19cd7bc06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d61da461-e738-5414-9b19-a2d59208047c" :answer/id #uuid "d61da461-e738-5414-9b19-a2d59208047c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4017e457-d6e9-5799-a313-b509dcbd530f" :question/id #uuid "4017e457-d6e9-5799-a313-b509dcbd530f" :question/math-academy-id "q-353047" :question/problem "The region enclosed by the curve $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=5$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/ad/ad089d39428498123469a2ac7895f7d04b8b3ff9dac18a1698462bfc7fabae99.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/97/975f68d06bb52e162ad792987899509f4090c03ec1b55af6335982a2951f6bbf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,5-(4-(x-1)^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(1+(x-1)^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+2(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}((x-1)^{4}+2(x-1)^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)^{3}+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}+2-(-\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}+0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{43}{15}+\\frac{13}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aada71ef-29a8-53e6-9657-ff4c532d0d90"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aada71ef-29a8-53e6-9657-ff4c532d0d90" :answer-field/id #uuid "aada71ef-29a8-53e6-9657-ff4c532d0d90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-400565d9-66a0-529c-8554-38d138f2516a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-400565d9-66a0-529c-8554-38d138f2516a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-400565d9-66a0-529c-8554-38d138f2516a" :answer/id #uuid "400565d9-66a0-529c-8554-38d138f2516a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{56\\pi}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b9b18aa-2c78-5504-8e8f-08d14250da87" :question/id #uuid "6b9b18aa-2c78-5504-8e8f-08d14250da87" :question/math-academy-id "q-85996" :question/problem "The region enclosed by the curve $y={e}^{x},$ the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1,$ and the horizontal line $y=3$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $y=3.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/72/72b701d2404e1acdcfba6b1994588419afc8246de6df8bf50e93baca0051df6a.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/89/89d6b6d2d65f161353da5fea9175aaf780f0fdc38405b855fbbc033349a484db.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={e}^{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(3)-({e}^{x})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(3-{e}^{x})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(9-6{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(9x-6{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ([9-6e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\text{ }]-[-6+\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{29}{2}-6e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(29-12e+{e}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5fb55632-1e14-5d4a-90a8-484b3e57cb27"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5fb55632-1e14-5d4a-90a8-484b3e57cb27" :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb55632-1e14-5d4a-90a8-484b3e57cb27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93ec2393-d1d4-5510-a6e4-d1bb9678dc84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-de381ab8-1a56-54a1-85c0-73ccdbfab087" "answer-93ec2393-d1d4-5510-a6e4-d1bb9678dc84" "answer-a721e4f2-439d-5540-be18-aff7f38c48f8" "answer-e9cc4d97-ff13-5bfb-89cd-cc7ea14e5f23" "answer-185c9f21-b220-5b11-8796-8a91d31e7570"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de381ab8-1a56-54a1-85c0-73ccdbfab087" :answer/id #uuid "de381ab8-1a56-54a1-85c0-73ccdbfab087" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(10+12e+2{e}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93ec2393-d1d4-5510-a6e4-d1bb9678dc84" :answer/id #uuid "93ec2393-d1d4-5510-a6e4-d1bb9678dc84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(29-12e+{e}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a721e4f2-439d-5540-be18-aff7f38c48f8" :answer/id #uuid "a721e4f2-439d-5540-be18-aff7f38c48f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(5+6e+{e}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9cc4d97-ff13-5bfb-89cd-cc7ea14e5f23" :answer/id #uuid "e9cc4d97-ff13-5bfb-89cd-cc7ea14e5f23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi (5+12e+{e}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-185c9f21-b220-5b11-8796-8a91d31e7570" :answer/id #uuid "185c9f21-b220-5b11-8796-8a91d31e7570" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi (5+6e+{e}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-48038fbf-e0e6-55ac-98d1-e8c48a8b376a" :question/id #uuid "48038fbf-e0e6-55ac-98d1-e8c48a8b376a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81661" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d0b7f238afe0f6cb82bb8670b9589aa10490b6e776e7f4a991a561aa0ad2245.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-x}$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ from $x=0$ to $x=3,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$about the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/73/73fc1e2cad0969a629e2c2c4a55ce8ac66c16b67fe2984e8b75dc6aa3a5eeace.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt{4-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,(\\sqrt{4-x})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}((4-x)-2\\sqrt{4-x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(5-x-2\\sqrt{4-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (5{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi (5x{|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{4}{3}(4-x)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((15-0)-(\\frac{9}{2}-0)+\\frac{4}{3}(1-8)) \\\\  & =\\pi (15-\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-94050dc1-3fc5-5684-9d2f-0b67b160855c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-94050dc1-3fc5-5684-9d2f-0b67b160855c" :answer-field/id #uuid "94050dc1-3fc5-5684-9d2f-0b67b160855c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-97675cbf-0281-5bcd-b979-29754bf68a38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14edbb70-481d-567e-9815-ccc321d95b20" "answer-b5467958-0603-5580-82c7-141e0c309a88" "answer-09506fd1-2815-56b2-9895-a8f2fa17df31" "answer-97675cbf-0281-5bcd-b979-29754bf68a38" "answer-d641d391-ba5a-5748-974b-53dbc8762112"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14edbb70-481d-567e-9815-ccc321d95b20" :answer/id #uuid "14edbb70-481d-567e-9815-ccc321d95b20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5467958-0603-5580-82c7-141e0c309a88" :answer/id #uuid "b5467958-0603-5580-82c7-141e0c309a88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09506fd1-2815-56b2-9895-a8f2fa17df31" :answer/id #uuid "09506fd1-2815-56b2-9895-a8f2fa17df31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97675cbf-0281-5bcd-b979-29754bf68a38" :answer/id #uuid "97675cbf-0281-5bcd-b979-29754bf68a38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d641d391-ba5a-5748-974b-53dbc8762112" :answer/id #uuid "d641d391-ba5a-5748-974b-53dbc8762112" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3fd041ce-cfe7-560a-8362-3b96b6047662" :question/id #uuid "3fd041ce-cfe7-560a-8362-3b96b6047662" :question/math-academy-id "q-352955" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e402d36061211a9e51df852f7fa595eb212e5c6ce9b3d43a2d53f9bea1fedd3.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=(x-1)^{4}$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/2a/2a2e4be26d18e408bf6e4580c72ec2f3c08eed1750d38e0716f06a32d3c03e7c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=(x-1)^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,1-(x-1)^{4}\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-2(x-1)^{4}+(x-1)^{8})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(x-\\frac{2}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{9}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([2-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(1)^{9}\\text{ }]-[0-\\frac{2}{5}(-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(-1)^{9}\\text{ }]) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68dd3cc2-e623-50ea-9f8e-1f4fb1b37990"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68dd3cc2-e623-50ea-9f8e-1f4fb1b37990" :answer-field/id #uuid "68dd3cc2-e623-50ea-9f8e-1f4fb1b37990" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-97b40c85-8993-522e-96cc-df161ffe20c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97b40c85-8993-522e-96cc-df161ffe20c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97b40c85-8993-522e-96cc-df161ffe20c9" :answer/id #uuid "97b40c85-8993-522e-96cc-df161ffe20c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi}{45}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-059b810f-7faa-5870-a600-8d8d6445cd02" :question/id #uuid "059b810f-7faa-5870-a600-8d8d6445cd02" :question/math-academy-id "q-120810" :question/problem "![](images/7e/7eb8812e90e4862a3658773186fe3809b212d58d5d22613fac6d9067ae4ccbc9.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the vertical line $x=e,$ and the horizontal line $y=0$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=e,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/f0/f0ddb5b21bdff9cbed426abed5bf9b94c4c4c4d742e2eb90dcdb92895c2adf3a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={e}^{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=e.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(e)-({e}^{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(e-{e}^{y})^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9de61be3-338b-5af9-988b-fcfbb09b5e3b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9de61be3-338b-5af9-988b-fcfbb09b5e3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "9de61be3-338b-5af9-988b-fcfbb09b5e3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-11fc6c5f-f3de-5a82-9ef6-baa130370ea3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed9ac4d7-eb0c-564c-af55-a27d0dcbfe73" "answer-cf4c31b1-5151-50a1-a311-e847ea06c5d7" "answer-11fc6c5f-f3de-5a82-9ef6-baa130370ea3" "answer-8f762836-19b7-5da7-9721-d9209f79d770" "answer-db1b9e49-00da-5316-bb71-7c891cad6f5d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed9ac4d7-eb0c-564c-af55-a27d0dcbfe73" :answer/id #uuid "ed9ac4d7-eb0c-564c-af55-a27d0dcbfe73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}+2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf4c31b1-5151-50a1-a311-e847ea06c5d7" :answer/id #uuid "cf4c31b1-5151-50a1-a311-e847ea06c5d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}+2{e}^{y}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11fc6c5f-f3de-5a82-9ef6-baa130370ea3" :answer/id #uuid "11fc6c5f-f3de-5a82-9ef6-baa130370ea3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f762836-19b7-5da7-9721-d9209f79d770" :answer/id #uuid "8f762836-19b7-5da7-9721-d9209f79d770" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}-2{e}^{y}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db1b9e49-00da-5316-bb71-7c891cad6f5d" :answer/id #uuid "db1b9e49-00da-5316-bb71-7c891cad6f5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-e)\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4fe06b9c-2dd8-5751-a283-a97db5419aa5" :question/id #uuid "4fe06b9c-2dd8-5751-a283-a97db5419aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-120816" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d154a914d58f5b695adec9dd873ac067ba8280a4fefffd4f2f5559ff5ee04da.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{1}{1-x},$ the vertical line $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=-2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=2,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\,⟹\\,x=1-\\frac{1}{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=-2$ to $y=-1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/23/239113c4d2bbca26c694796f6f17bd26bebf47697dad53f7af832088213fd4c9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1-\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{[\\,(2)-(1-\\frac{1}{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{(1+\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d3236f7-ef4e-5824-b7ca-2c3a37165b7a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d3236f7-ef4e-5824-b7ca-2c3a37165b7a" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3236f7-ef4e-5824-b7ca-2c3a37165b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6dfeeed5-c339-5e20-92cf-869495d75216" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c716c79-f420-5422-84b3-0336b564a145" "answer-6dfeeed5-c339-5e20-92cf-869495d75216" "answer-cc893279-f68c-5b8a-aa8a-10a3e7137e89" "answer-ef803d29-9d1e-5966-824f-0510361b80aa" "answer-5f06fca2-f629-5be7-a4a3-1f4853dc03eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c716c79-f420-5422-84b3-0336b564a145" :answer/id #uuid "3c716c79-f420-5422-84b3-0336b564a145" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dfeeed5-c339-5e20-92cf-869495d75216" :answer/id #uuid "6dfeeed5-c339-5e20-92cf-869495d75216" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc893279-f68c-5b8a-aa8a-10a3e7137e89" :answer/id #uuid "cc893279-f68c-5b8a-aa8a-10a3e7137e89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{4}{y}+\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef803d29-9d1e-5966-824f-0510361b80aa" :answer/id #uuid "ef803d29-9d1e-5966-824f-0510361b80aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f06fca2-f629-5be7-a4a3-1f4853dc03eb" :answer/id #uuid "5f06fca2-f629-5be7-a4a3-1f4853dc03eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1-\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b4260f61-3124-52b4-9558-94f62f160010" :question/id #uuid "b4260f61-3124-52b4-9558-94f62f160010" :question/math-academy-id "q-50017" :question/problem "![](images/29/293593a043a7713d87a2f8ad8a2eaecee26b34288e4662bb4b2fce834e703e56.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ the vertical line $x=-1$ and the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/60/600ef6d7265add5c669a29394e45facae040292ce19caad9dcae390979b604b9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt[3]{y})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt[3]{y}+1)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da2b25ef-5cc5-53ca-8aad-45786fb6f09c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da2b25ef-5cc5-53ca-8aad-45786fb6f09c" :answer-field/id #uuid "da2b25ef-5cc5-53ca-8aad-45786fb6f09c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ff41c014-8136-5e89-92d6-465efca4119d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1e18ec7-b86d-52c4-a800-15588ea1dbf3" "answer-e76bc17f-0baf-5d2a-9985-1a028ff25f28" "answer-e58d8772-610c-564c-9aa1-9a6eaf4c3487" "answer-d2988696-3e8e-5d85-a5e3-6f62050554f4" "answer-ff41c014-8136-5e89-92d6-465efca4119d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1e18ec7-b86d-52c4-a800-15588ea1dbf3" :answer/id #uuid "d1e18ec7-b86d-52c4-a800-15588ea1dbf3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2{y}^{1/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e76bc17f-0baf-5d2a-9985-1a028ff25f28" :answer/id #uuid "e76bc17f-0baf-5d2a-9985-1a028ff25f28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e58d8772-610c-564c-9aa1-9a6eaf4c3487" :answer/id #uuid "e58d8772-610c-564c-9aa1-9a6eaf4c3487" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2988696-3e8e-5d85-a5e3-6f62050554f4" :answer/id #uuid "d2988696-3e8e-5d85-a5e3-6f62050554f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff41c014-8136-5e89-92d6-465efca4119d" :answer/id #uuid "ff41c014-8136-5e89-92d6-465efca4119d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-27279421-a932-5375-bd1a-4824ccac1d67" :question/id #uuid "27279421-a932-5375-bd1a-4824ccac1d67" :question/math-academy-id "q-120837" :question/problem "![](images/68/68359143b50b4d6187f2d50793b1d3cb4b48656f1996cd222cb5d07f666b535e.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=-1-x,$ the vertical line $x=-3,$ and the horizontal line $y=0$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-3,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=-1-x\\,⟹\\,x=-1-y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=-3$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1-y & =-3 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/73/73d5da7e1bd4b409574f6e9ab034dde51012eb956b3ea72a3529f7d432fe781d.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=-1-y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(-1-y)-(-3)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-y)}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-57561582-2db8-5679-9544-434bd74cb0e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57561582-2db8-5679-9544-434bd74cb0e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "57561582-2db8-5679-9544-434bd74cb0e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-21d23244-e732-5433-957b-488175409ab8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f311bd18-39bc-5966-bcd6-64f303891d4e" "answer-21d23244-e732-5433-957b-488175409ab8" "answer-7f4f38a5-4afe-53b3-8d55-92144694484d" "answer-17ee6b3d-079f-5a5f-87b1-7015ef18baa2" "answer-10d86b88-e994-55d8-9aab-8c7074f2fa73"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f311bd18-39bc-5966-bcd6-64f303891d4e" :answer/id #uuid "f311bd18-39bc-5966-bcd6-64f303891d4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-y)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21d23244-e732-5433-957b-488175409ab8" :answer/id #uuid "21d23244-e732-5433-957b-488175409ab8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f4f38a5-4afe-53b3-8d55-92144694484d" :answer/id #uuid "7f4f38a5-4afe-53b3-8d55-92144694484d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3/2}({y}^{2}+4y+4)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17ee6b3d-079f-5a5f-87b1-7015ef18baa2" :answer/id #uuid "17ee6b3d-079f-5a5f-87b1-7015ef18baa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+2y+2)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10d86b88-e994-55d8-9aab-8c7074f2fa73" :answer/id #uuid "10d86b88-e994-55d8-9aab-8c7074f2fa73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}({y}^{2}-y+2)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a978de85-c2b4-579a-92f4-5064fe99c547" :question/id #uuid "a978de85-c2b4-579a-92f4-5064fe99c547" :question/math-academy-id "q-50081" :question/problem "![](images/42/421d588cd290294cf6471704f2459c22b00c8b47756b808e9da267a8007792b2.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=2,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe take the positive sign since $x\\ge 0.$\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}} & =2 \\\\ y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=8$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/c7/c762c0e41cf8862535e5241a00149fdc6e409c3f79630a411cff73fcd11759b0.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}{[\\,(2)-(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}{(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}-2)}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(\\frac{y}{2}-4\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88f2c76e-9efc-51e9-af76-e42ebea84266"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88f2c76e-9efc-51e9-af76-e42ebea84266" :answer-field/id #uuid "88f2c76e-9efc-51e9-af76-e42ebea84266" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7bdd43ab-c905-5c98-9b3a-08fb054e4d7c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63401029-dbe2-52a7-bcb8-3a4927a7cf88" "answer-259de651-fd50-5b82-8e61-2db5fc5258f6" "answer-090e8a68-e8fa-5bc8-90fd-9cd2d69e4b1a" "answer-8e437008-9a45-55bd-a385-ef285947ff5f" "answer-7bdd43ab-c905-5c98-9b3a-08fb054e4d7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63401029-dbe2-52a7-bcb8-3a4927a7cf88" :answer/id #uuid "63401029-dbe2-52a7-bcb8-3a4927a7cf88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{y}{4}-2\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-259de651-fd50-5b82-8e61-2db5fc5258f6" :answer/id #uuid "259de651-fd50-5b82-8e61-2db5fc5258f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}-2)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-090e8a68-e8fa-5bc8-90fd-9cd2d69e4b1a" :answer/id #uuid "090e8a68-e8fa-5bc8-90fd-9cd2d69e4b1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(4\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e437008-9a45-55bd-a385-ef285947ff5f" :answer/id #uuid "8e437008-9a45-55bd-a385-ef285947ff5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(\\frac{y}{2}+2)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bdd43ab-c905-5c98-9b3a-08fb054e4d7c" :answer/id #uuid "7bdd43ab-c905-5c98-9b3a-08fb054e4d7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(\\frac{y}{2}-4\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0ccddc8e-6343-5b87-aa99-4a041586cf69" :question/id #uuid "0ccddc8e-6343-5b87-aa99-4a041586cf69" :question/math-academy-id "q-86058" :question/problem "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt[3]{y},$ the vertical line $x=3,$ and the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/47/47b612a3e08ff24055cbffa7d53fb337f1905ec861abd3a8e535088d383162cb.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/19/1927874f5eca0c18b7b61b37d0e6fac5198ec9dcf89205763e88570287330292.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt[3]{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(3)-(\\sqrt[3]{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3-\\sqrt[3]{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}-6{y}^{1/3}+9)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}y+9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{3{y}^{5/3}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{9{y}^{4/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}+9y{|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{3}{5}-0)-(\\frac{9}{2}-0)+(9-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{51\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27369d98-76f7-5b69-8322-ea2ff0fb9031"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27369d98-76f7-5b69-8322-ea2ff0fb9031" :answer-field/id #uuid "27369d98-76f7-5b69-8322-ea2ff0fb9031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-08767496-af57-5656-a9a1-caa61817cc13" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8281b9a5-0a80-578b-871f-663a9780da04" "answer-08767496-af57-5656-a9a1-caa61817cc13" "answer-96f4ffa4-c0a8-5dc0-b1e0-7f339689319c" "answer-aa34b084-e5f8-5ab6-9e0d-5461e1ea38c2" "answer-68ce0533-1775-50fc-a2d1-e656f5328e27"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8281b9a5-0a80-578b-871f-663a9780da04" :answer/id #uuid "8281b9a5-0a80-578b-871f-663a9780da04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08767496-af57-5656-a9a1-caa61817cc13" :answer/id #uuid "08767496-af57-5656-a9a1-caa61817cc13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{51\\pi }{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96f4ffa4-c0a8-5dc0-b1e0-7f339689319c" :answer/id #uuid "96f4ffa4-c0a8-5dc0-b1e0-7f339689319c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{51\\pi }{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa34b084-e5f8-5ab6-9e0d-5461e1ea38c2" :answer/id #uuid "aa34b084-e5f8-5ab6-9e0d-5461e1ea38c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68ce0533-1775-50fc-a2d1-e656f5328e27" :answer/id #uuid "68ce0533-1775-50fc-a2d1-e656f5328e27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-08dd2009-a210-5f29-bbaa-81488a5664f6" :question/id #uuid "08dd2009-a210-5f29-bbaa-81488a5664f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-353068" :question/problem "![](images/54/5453dfc44e11cad377c1e8e2c30fc23d537d4423d38fab4428395362f51d9412.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}+1,$ the vertical line $x=5,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=5.$\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersection of the curve and the line $x=5$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+1 & =5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/e8/e83c5729ef5db920a0b451a8571ad84f45d2b4e950ab831ced0da2c0a12cea50.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={y}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,5-({y}^{2}+1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(16-8{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(16y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (32-\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-02b568fb-76bc-5e15-9c73-e0b3712cc107"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-02b568fb-76bc-5e15-9c73-e0b3712cc107" :answer-field/id #uuid "02b568fb-76bc-5e15-9c73-e0b3712cc107" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f569a1f0-e362-5804-81cd-4cb22377f499" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f569a1f0-e362-5804-81cd-4cb22377f499"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f569a1f0-e362-5804-81cd-4cb22377f499" :answer/id #uuid "f569a1f0-e362-5804-81cd-4cb22377f499" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{256\\pi}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2588c644-3085-550f-abbf-182631d5c4e8" :question/id #uuid "2588c644-3085-550f-abbf-182631d5c4e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-120906" :question/problem "![](images/14/144259aa150936de0e3425c13f953da0833d87c2aba1304aa39b3c47fda03550.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1,$ the horizontal line $y=1,$  the horizontal line $y=0,$ and the vertical line $x=-1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-1$, as shown above. Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left function and which is the right, let's draw our horizontal arrow.\n\n![](images/8a/8a17502c300fae56e9f2e1706d23a3e5a7d3233eebff9a77958d117a0135adb0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the resulting solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(6\\sqrt{y}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{y}+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24\\sqrt{y}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24{y}^{1/2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(36\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+24\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}+4y)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(18(1)^{2}+16(1)^{3/2}+4(1))-0] \\\\  & =38\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3324bc9b-8a8d-5b4e-82c5-238fdef233a3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3324bc9b-8a8d-5b4e-82c5-238fdef233a3" :answer-field/id #uuid "3324bc9b-8a8d-5b4e-82c5-238fdef233a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7e634175-26e2-5b33-932f-02da2cc68430" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14f86113-90f2-58e5-9e9a-3d5f87a4056e" "answer-be4c2add-16e0-5c1d-96be-00048e79502c" "answer-7e634175-26e2-5b33-932f-02da2cc68430" "answer-456bc927-97e0-508b-bce6-ec15a7546b24" "answer-15e87c4b-5a56-5f0a-9604-0d35fbeb9425"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14f86113-90f2-58e5-9e9a-3d5f87a4056e" :answer/id #uuid "14f86113-90f2-58e5-9e9a-3d5f87a4056e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be4c2add-16e0-5c1d-96be-00048e79502c" :answer/id #uuid "be4c2add-16e0-5c1d-96be-00048e79502c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e634175-26e2-5b33-932f-02da2cc68430" :answer/id #uuid "7e634175-26e2-5b33-932f-02da2cc68430" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "38\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-456bc927-97e0-508b-bce6-ec15a7546b24" :answer/id #uuid "456bc927-97e0-508b-bce6-ec15a7546b24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15e87c4b-5a56-5f0a-9604-0d35fbeb9425" :answer/id #uuid "15e87c4b-5a56-5f0a-9604-0d35fbeb9425" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6ffaec2e-3741-5b7e-88f5-bdff517c9f5d" :question/id #uuid "6ffaec2e-3741-5b7e-88f5-bdff517c9f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81786" :question/problem "![](images/46/4643824b623d5325f53407147f086a95073db91be0148381780668c420ec7c45.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={e}^{y},$ the vertical line $x=e,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=e$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =e \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/93/93fd0875ea2ba1d1d92bb781a61cfb114ac88b7d240cd825b4842f686f7c760f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={e}^{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=e.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(e)-({e}^{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[e-{e}^{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2y})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y+1}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{y+1}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\pi (({e}^{2}-0)-2({e}^{2}-e)+(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\pi (-{e}^{2}+2e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4e-{e}^{2}-1)\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(-1+4e-{e}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b227d07-d337-5e85-81ba-0cc9d053f147"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b227d07-d337-5e85-81ba-0cc9d053f147" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b227d07-d337-5e85-81ba-0cc9d053f147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bac171fe-ef9e-5229-9b88-446a005d22bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6452e493-c57a-5fe5-a1cf-953bef5c9e6f" "answer-f841f37f-0274-5bed-9030-b86472ded932" "answer-bac171fe-ef9e-5229-9b88-446a005d22bc" "answer-af3dfa5f-81db-523c-b026-a7f56854f0cf" "answer-55110426-9b6c-5147-8c18-32fcf01860b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6452e493-c57a-5fe5-a1cf-953bef5c9e6f" :answer/id #uuid "6452e493-c57a-5fe5-a1cf-953bef5c9e6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi e}{1+4{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f841f37f-0274-5bed-9030-b86472ded932" :answer/id #uuid "f841f37f-0274-5bed-9030-b86472ded932" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi e}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bac171fe-ef9e-5229-9b88-446a005d22bc" :answer/id #uuid "bac171fe-ef9e-5229-9b88-446a005d22bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(-1+4e-{e}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af3dfa5f-81db-523c-b026-a7f56854f0cf" :answer/id #uuid "af3dfa5f-81db-523c-b026-a7f56854f0cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(16-{e}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55110426-9b6c-5147-8c18-32fcf01860b7" :answer/id #uuid "55110426-9b6c-5147-8c18-32fcf01860b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(4-e)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f10ec35-272f-563f-8af5-2c0fc2e74c5d" :question/id #uuid "2f10ec35-272f-563f-8af5-2c0fc2e74c5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353061" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d8f2d52a0d943a4eeb1d323cc90db145c9b87b57e87d595a7ca5809498e7a6a.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x=y+1,$ the vertical line $x=4,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=4.$\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersection of the curve and the line $x=4$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =4 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=3$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/f9/f9fd1773b9599b1a4b61fc58c854bf998ed147a9707f719a80a3588874c0eb4f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=y+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[4-(y+1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}(3-y)^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-{3}^{3}) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3e380c07-676e-5d1a-a447-464aebaba18c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3e380c07-676e-5d1a-a447-464aebaba18c" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e380c07-676e-5d1a-a447-464aebaba18c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8f256f46-100c-5687-961a-0d3ebef98f64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f256f46-100c-5687-961a-0d3ebef98f64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f256f46-100c-5687-961a-0d3ebef98f64" :answer/id #uuid "8f256f46-100c-5687-961a-0d3ebef98f64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116407 :tutorial/content "Consider the region bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the horizontal line $y=1,$ and the vertical line $x=3,$ as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/99/990769dd023de72d2cc4caf3e71d161f6af0605962eb20c8a0d71e67a6ff9b95.png)\n\nLet's rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the line $y=1$ to get the solid shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/e1/e12187d889e7389c4d05e5e91245f555aab4e1c2395ef05168cdf0e3f13b6fe3.png)\n\nWe can find the volume using the disc method:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\left[ r(x) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nHere, $r(x)$ is the radius of the disc that corresponds to each point $x$ under consideration. \n\n**Watch out!** Since the axis of revolution is $y=1$ (not $y=0$), we need to account for the change in the corresponding discs' radius. \n\nThe radius is *not* just $\\sqrt{x}.$ Rather, the radius is the distance between the line $y=1$ and the function $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ \n\n![](images/d3/d33fec84ada995fe3593132ae1567c591c030ed458e5cd923180bc2204f4fb4e.png)\n\nTo find an expression for the radius, we can take the difference between the upper function and the lower function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function}) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{x})-(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express the volume as the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{(\\sqrt{x}-1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116408 :tutorial/content "Consider the region bounded by the curve $y=x^2,$ the vertical line $x=1,$ and the horizontal line $y=4,$ as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/67/67b5f2a387c6892846ed063934aa3de66cab70410b00741cb3b3c32561a0f9e7.png)\n\nLet's rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the line $x=1$ to get the solid shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f367888095347c2da4173ec97e91df7057c9149d6eab0d7f1a2b62f9180c3a5.png)\n\nTo find the volume of this solid, we can use the disc method, as usual:\n\n$$\nV =  \\pi \\int_{c}^{d} \\left[ r(y) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that the radius $r(y)$ is now horizontal rather than vertical.\n\n![](images/3c/3c9a33c1e3a55e900185002580319af5015cb5b2e0f876228910a98ab42399fa.png)\n\nTo find an expression for the radius, we compute the difference between the right and left functions.\n\n- The right function corresponds to the curve $y=x^2.$ Solving for $x,$ we get $x = \\pm \\sqrt y.$ The region of interest is in the first quadrant, where $x$ is positive, so we must have $x = \\sqrt{y}.$\n\n- The left function corresponds to the vertical line $x=1.$\n\nSo, the radius is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(y) & =(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function}) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{y})-(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is represented by the integral\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_1^4 \\left( \\sqrt{y} - 1 \\right)^2 \\,\\textrm{d}y \\, .\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115453 :activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116181 :step/math-academy-id 646717 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116212 :step/math-academy-id 646718 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116182 :step/math-academy-id 646721 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116183 :step/math-academy-id 646724 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116184 :step/math-academy-id 646725 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116213 :step/math-academy-id 646728 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068807 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bb2d031b-f14f-51f1-86a8-2bb2273a329c" "question-813ed141-a0ad-5559-9d01-1e28afeea83d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb2d031b-f14f-51f1-86a8-2bb2273a329c" :question/id #uuid "bb2d031b-f14f-51f1-86a8-2bb2273a329c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89851" :question/problem "![](images/19/19c55ecaf73ac1c0c8db619e6c43d14f35e50e2321ccae5687b3a1bc6a687563.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph I has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-19bb8e3c-21b6-517b-aa91-0a2468777551"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19bb8e3c-21b6-517b-aa91-0a2468777551" :answer-field/id #uuid "19bb8e3c-21b6-517b-aa91-0a2468777551" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fc3c2d8b-4741-5de4-bebc-838131f9c433" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a21b775b-e252-5062-a83b-24ddc437bd27" "answer-2505ec9c-7b5f-512f-a7e5-688e8971a739" "answer-fc3c2d8b-4741-5de4-bebc-838131f9c433" "answer-7c3893a0-6fa2-54d0-9dad-5ef0f1f4fa0b" "answer-20d3d367-ef61-5f0e-b028-76b8d02b1bff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a21b775b-e252-5062-a83b-24ddc437bd27" :answer/id #uuid "a21b775b-e252-5062-a83b-24ddc437bd27" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2505ec9c-7b5f-512f-a7e5-688e8971a739" :answer/id #uuid "2505ec9c-7b5f-512f-a7e5-688e8971a739" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc3c2d8b-4741-5de4-bebc-838131f9c433" :answer/id #uuid "fc3c2d8b-4741-5de4-bebc-838131f9c433" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c3893a0-6fa2-54d0-9dad-5ef0f1f4fa0b" :answer/id #uuid "7c3893a0-6fa2-54d0-9dad-5ef0f1f4fa0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20d3d367-ef61-5f0e-b028-76b8d02b1bff" :answer/id #uuid "20d3d367-ef61-5f0e-b028-76b8d02b1bff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068808 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-96f2566c-9717-5c2b-9e76-8782952293d7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-96f2566c-9717-5c2b-9e76-8782952293d7" :question/id #uuid "96f2566c-9717-5c2b-9e76-8782952293d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-89857" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the second and fourth quadrants\n\nII. The range of $f(x)$ is $y\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- Statement II is false since the range of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec6ef3f5-a1e6-5276-9762-cc4e0cecbe3a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec6ef3f5-a1e6-5276-9762-cc4e0cecbe3a" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec6ef3f5-a1e6-5276-9762-cc4e0cecbe3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f5d103d-b016-56a7-843d-168644066425" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e37c7008-ad00-5155-ba60-4410a81c6120" "answer-89cbec0e-d997-5f98-9e31-5a809bd2b76d" "answer-f301abba-55eb-5980-9f33-5bed3c4ff731" "answer-4dc5b7f5-341f-5517-9ff3-798e626c786a" "answer-2f5d103d-b016-56a7-843d-168644066425"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e37c7008-ad00-5155-ba60-4410a81c6120" :answer/id #uuid "e37c7008-ad00-5155-ba60-4410a81c6120" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89cbec0e-d997-5f98-9e31-5a809bd2b76d" :answer/id #uuid "89cbec0e-d997-5f98-9e31-5a809bd2b76d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f301abba-55eb-5980-9f33-5bed3c4ff731" :answer/id #uuid "f301abba-55eb-5980-9f33-5bed3c4ff731" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dc5b7f5-341f-5517-9ff3-798e626c786a" :answer/id #uuid "4dc5b7f5-341f-5517-9ff3-798e626c786a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f5d103d-b016-56a7-843d-168644066425" :answer/id #uuid "2f5d103d-b016-56a7-843d-168644066425" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-813ed141-a0ad-5559-9d01-1e28afeea83d" :question/id #uuid "813ed141-a0ad-5559-9d01-1e28afeea83d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102954" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b0ed011d85165937593e0cc86439f0d26ab61e703c5c74ac824e10a5cfa4adf.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph IV has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ebe4757-b367-5836-98de-3d05256c7715"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ebe4757-b367-5836-98de-3d05256c7715" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ebe4757-b367-5836-98de-3d05256c7715" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-73fc0c4a-8712-5f5a-89bc-7a0b89357dbe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-37b43b95-6b54-5507-9e71-fdcfce468f59" "answer-d47e8365-50ab-501c-9600-aa054b401dca" "answer-562ab408-894e-5dbf-aef4-4c0a59f1145d" "answer-97c8ee38-8b13-5f9e-a76e-7584f65b43cf" "answer-73fc0c4a-8712-5f5a-89bc-7a0b89357dbe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37b43b95-6b54-5507-9e71-fdcfce468f59" :answer/id #uuid "37b43b95-6b54-5507-9e71-fdcfce468f59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d47e8365-50ab-501c-9600-aa054b401dca" :answer/id #uuid "d47e8365-50ab-501c-9600-aa054b401dca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-562ab408-894e-5dbf-aef4-4c0a59f1145d" :answer/id #uuid "562ab408-894e-5dbf-aef4-4c0a59f1145d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97c8ee38-8b13-5f9e-a76e-7584f65b43cf" :answer/id #uuid "97c8ee38-8b13-5f9e-a76e-7584f65b43cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73fc0c4a-8712-5f5a-89bc-7a0b89357dbe" :answer/id #uuid "73fc0c4a-8712-5f5a-89bc-7a0b89357dbe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-bfa6cfc3-6c41-5279-9f6d-aa2065b8862a" :activity/id #uuid "bfa6cfc3-6c41-5279-9f6d-aa2065b8862a" :activity/title "Graphing the Cube Root Function" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049528 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-96827c26-81d2-5373-98e3-81b7df5b858e" "step-d14be241-c405-5a69-ac0a-3d7de482d105" "step-3d1470c8-ba27-52a6-a01f-3373f12de77d"] :activity/first-step "step-96827c26-81d2-5373-98e3-81b7df5b858e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-96827c26-81d2-5373-98e3-81b7df5b858e" :step/id #uuid "96827c26-81d2-5373-98e3-81b7df5b858e" :step/content "question-bb2d031b-f14f-51f1-86a8-2bb2273a329c" :step/next "step-d14be241-c405-5a69-ac0a-3d7de482d105" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d14be241-c405-5a69-ac0a-3d7de482d105" :step/id #uuid "d14be241-c405-5a69-ac0a-3d7de482d105" :step/content "question-96f2566c-9717-5c2b-9e76-8782952293d7" :step/next "step-3d1470c8-ba27-52a6-a01f-3373f12de77d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3d1470c8-ba27-52a6-a01f-3373f12de77d" :step/id #uuid "3d1470c8-ba27-52a6-a01f-3373f12de77d" :step/content "question-813ed141-a0ad-5559-9d01-1e28afeea83d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068720 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7b6428c3-ddc3-50c7-8b44-d4c45bc81ecd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069298 :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a2fe4ad55bbc2853ce6f0507780292ae01616df0a2595ff108e674eb91b2557.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}?$" :question/worked-solution "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates ${{(4,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(8,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively. \n\nTo find the distance $AB,$ we can use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8-4)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066958 :answer-field/correct 17592186066959 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069299 :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-9,2)$ and $(-12,-4)?$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have ${{(-9,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-12,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12-(-9))^{2}+(-4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066964 :answer-field/correct 17592186066968 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b6428c3-ddc3-50c7-8b44-d4c45bc81ecd" :question/id #uuid "7b6428c3-ddc3-50c7-8b44-d4c45bc81ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-3720" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a225129d0879af950e935d794c09f3b479434154757a643c29c44a9d9e981e2.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{DE}?$" :question/worked-solution "From the figure, we note that the points $D$ and $E$ have coordinates ${{(-4,0)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(0,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$  respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{DE},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-(-4))^{2}+(3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-50de03fc-f361-5d04-9b42-75d709112b35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-50de03fc-f361-5d04-9b42-75d709112b35" :answer-field/id #uuid "50de03fc-f361-5d04-9b42-75d709112b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0eec1ef8-9fdc-5230-9417-7755a8d0b87e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43e972e7-cf56-5b6c-b604-0698d249e7cb" "answer-9dda4d19-a039-5a94-a7cf-7a1838c89803" "answer-0f8e6dec-43af-5f80-ab1e-3eef631db8bf" "answer-e6e5abdb-b91e-58f8-a040-8654f0c602a4" "answer-0eec1ef8-9fdc-5230-9417-7755a8d0b87e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43e972e7-cf56-5b6c-b604-0698d249e7cb" :answer/id #uuid "43e972e7-cf56-5b6c-b604-0698d249e7cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dda4d19-a039-5a94-a7cf-7a1838c89803" :answer/id #uuid "9dda4d19-a039-5a94-a7cf-7a1838c89803" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f8e6dec-43af-5f80-ab1e-3eef631db8bf" :answer/id #uuid "0f8e6dec-43af-5f80-ab1e-3eef631db8bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6e5abdb-b91e-58f8-a040-8654f0c602a4" :answer/id #uuid "e6e5abdb-b91e-58f8-a040-8654f0c602a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0eec1ef8-9fdc-5230-9417-7755a8d0b87e" :answer/id #uuid "0eec1ef8-9fdc-5230-9417-7755a8d0b87e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f2d4f687-8d6d-557d-aff5-81bf78a9d509" :activity/id #uuid "f2d4f687-8d6d-557d-aff5-81bf78a9d509" :activity/title "The Distance Formula" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050975 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-055f6147-a057-5f62-8e99-02d25958931d" "step-51636569-c0a8-5056-918a-d7101ba9f350" "step-d14f69d9-fc47-5d93-94d4-48dc6fa8a3a8"] :activity/first-step "step-055f6147-a057-5f62-8e99-02d25958931d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-055f6147-a057-5f62-8e99-02d25958931d" :step/id #uuid "055f6147-a057-5f62-8e99-02d25958931d" :step/content 17592186069298 :step/next "step-51636569-c0a8-5056-918a-d7101ba9f350" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-51636569-c0a8-5056-918a-d7101ba9f350" :step/id #uuid "51636569-c0a8-5056-918a-d7101ba9f350" :step/content 17592186069299 :step/next "step-d14f69d9-fc47-5d93-94d4-48dc6fa8a3a8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d14f69d9-fc47-5d93-94d4-48dc6fa8a3a8" :step/id #uuid "d14f69d9-fc47-5d93-94d4-48dc6fa8a3a8" :step/content "question-7b6428c3-ddc3-50c7-8b44-d4c45bc81ecd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078504 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-581fcb98-ffbb-541a-98a8-b1aad8f2210c" "question-0ad91671-88c2-5e5d-8e1f-e5e0e6fbe489"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-581fcb98-ffbb-541a-98a8-b1aad8f2210c" :question/id #uuid "581fcb98-ffbb-541a-98a8-b1aad8f2210c" :question/math-academy-id "q-7220" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨2,2⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(-10,-8)$ and has velocity $⟨0,0⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+{C}_{1},\\,2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨2(3)+{C}_{1},\\,2(3)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨6+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=-6.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t-6,\\,2t-6⟩.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t-6,\\,2t-6⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-6t+{C}_{3},\\,{t}^{2}-6t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨-10,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨(3)^{2}-6(3)+{C}_{3},\\,(3)^{2}-6(3)+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-10,-8⟩ & =⟨-9+{C}_{3},\\,-9+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=-1$ and ${C}_{4}=1.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-6t-1,\\,{t}^{2}-6t+1⟩.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73458c53-8f94-50f4-87a0-6b7b8f52ee68"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73458c53-8f94-50f4-87a0-6b7b8f52ee68" :answer-field/id #uuid "73458c53-8f94-50f4-87a0-6b7b8f52ee68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-73c2b755-2071-5c0c-9b6a-6f55d2c24877" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8196c611-8269-5b46-aaab-fcda97fa7905" "answer-d06485ea-0969-5fee-ab0d-205e477ec3d5" "answer-e1a93bdc-1551-59df-9664-dd4c01781ef6" "answer-73c2b755-2071-5c0c-9b6a-6f55d2c24877" "answer-0f750c9c-9563-5dff-b76b-6cdab6489c2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8196c611-8269-5b46-aaab-fcda97fa7905" :answer/id #uuid "8196c611-8269-5b46-aaab-fcda97fa7905" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{2}-4t+1,\\,{t}^{2}-4t+1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d06485ea-0969-5fee-ab0d-205e477ec3d5" :answer/id #uuid "d06485ea-0969-5fee-ab0d-205e477ec3d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{2}-4t-1,\\,{t}^{2}-4t+1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1a93bdc-1551-59df-9664-dd4c01781ef6" :answer/id #uuid "e1a93bdc-1551-59df-9664-dd4c01781ef6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{2}-4t-3,\\,{t}^{2}-4t+3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73c2b755-2071-5c0c-9b6a-6f55d2c24877" :answer/id #uuid "73c2b755-2071-5c0c-9b6a-6f55d2c24877" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{2}-6t-1,\\,{t}^{2}-6t+1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f750c9c-9563-5dff-b76b-6cdab6489c2c" :answer/id #uuid "0f750c9c-9563-5dff-b76b-6cdab6489c2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{2}-6t-3,\\,{t}^{2}-6t+3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078485 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d9799b8b-4af9-5ece-b36d-864fdc8c4517" "question-e49b201b-78f0-5668-9145-a0f21b5a41c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d9799b8b-4af9-5ece-b36d-864fdc8c4517" :question/id #uuid "d9799b8b-4af9-5ece-b36d-864fdc8c4517" :question/math-academy-id "q-50514" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t-2,2t⟩\\,,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $r=⟨-4,-8⟩$ when $t=1,$ find the times when the particle is at the origin." :question/worked-solution "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=⟨0,0⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t-2,2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (2t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-2t+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-4,\\,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨(1)^{2}-2(1)+{C}_{1},\\,(1)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,\\,-8⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-3$ and ${C}_{2}=-9.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-2t-3,\\,{t}^{2}-9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{2}-2t-3,\\,{t}^{2}-9⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2t-3=(t-3)(t+1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1,3, \\\\ {t}^{2}-9=(t+3)(t-3) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52d8812b-e4f6-535d-8703-947e3c409cdb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52d8812b-e4f6-535d-8703-947e3c409cdb" :answer-field/id #uuid "52d8812b-e4f6-535d-8703-947e3c409cdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-76d77a6a-9075-58d1-96c5-7903feb7ab81" :answer-field/choices ["answer-975ce232-2b73-5609-a251-e2146288e6c9" "answer-1f4d3e58-1a06-55b3-8d8a-253f122c2f7a" "answer-76d77a6a-9075-58d1-96c5-7903feb7ab81" "answer-3b11ce37-8ee8-5667-b95d-c6c40475adaf" "answer-7306054b-0128-5b0d-b3f4-b15c499deeaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-975ce232-2b73-5609-a251-e2146288e6c9" :answer/id #uuid "975ce232-2b73-5609-a251-e2146288e6c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f4d3e58-1a06-55b3-8d8a-253f122c2f7a" :answer/id #uuid "1f4d3e58-1a06-55b3-8d8a-253f122c2f7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76d77a6a-9075-58d1-96c5-7903feb7ab81" :answer/id #uuid "76d77a6a-9075-58d1-96c5-7903feb7ab81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b11ce37-8ee8-5667-b95d-c6c40475adaf" :answer/id #uuid "3b11ce37-8ee8-5667-b95d-c6c40475adaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7306054b-0128-5b0d-b3f4-b15c499deeaa" :answer/id #uuid "7306054b-0128-5b0d-b3f4-b15c499deeaa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The particle is never at the origin" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0ad91671-88c2-5e5d-8e1f-e5e0e6fbe489" :question/id #uuid "0ad91671-88c2-5e5d-8e1f-e5e0e6fbe489" :question/math-academy-id "q-122473" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨12{t}^{2},-{e}^{-t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(-1,-4)$ and has velocity $⟨0,1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨12{t}^{2},-{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 12{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},{e}^{-t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨4({0}^{3})+{C}_{1},{e}^{-0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨4{t}^{3},{e}^{-t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{t}^{3},{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}+{C}_{3},-{e}^{-t}+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-1,-4⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{4}+{C}_{3},-{e}^{-0}+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-4⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=-1$ and ${C}_{4}=-3.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{4}-1,-{e}^{-t}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5d207ef-09f8-5f25-8bd3-232e40b6944c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5d207ef-09f8-5f25-8bd3-232e40b6944c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d207ef-09f8-5f25-8bd3-232e40b6944c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a9bffbe1-0ad0-5e0b-9f4c-ef4ebf05851f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-04cb33d6-59ad-5314-b00b-9e2eb864c2c1" "answer-09cc6717-a213-5f9b-b691-d2184fbd9f56" "answer-eae18b35-0d1e-5dff-bc1d-614ad89c032c" "answer-a9bffbe1-0ad0-5e0b-9f4c-ef4ebf05851f" "answer-79627921-0fbf-53b2-af26-839975422f40"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04cb33d6-59ad-5314-b00b-9e2eb864c2c1" :answer/id #uuid "04cb33d6-59ad-5314-b00b-9e2eb864c2c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=⟨-4{t}^{4}-1,{e}^{-t}-5⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09cc6717-a213-5f9b-b691-d2184fbd9f56" :answer/id #uuid "09cc6717-a213-5f9b-b691-d2184fbd9f56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2{t}^{4}-1,-{e}^{-t}-3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eae18b35-0d1e-5dff-bc1d-614ad89c032c" :answer/id #uuid "eae18b35-0d1e-5dff-bc1d-614ad89c032c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2{t}^{4}-1,2{e}^{-t}-6⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9bffbe1-0ad0-5e0b-9f4c-ef4ebf05851f" :answer/id #uuid "a9bffbe1-0ad0-5e0b-9f4c-ef4ebf05851f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-1,-{e}^{-t}-3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79627921-0fbf-53b2-af26-839975422f40" :answer/id #uuid "79627921-0fbf-53b2-af26-839975422f40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-1,{e}^{-t}-5⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078484 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-990e6c9c-e46f-5a33-8086-74712d90c327"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-990e6c9c-e46f-5a33-8086-74712d90c327" :question/id #uuid "990e6c9c-e46f-5a33-8086-74712d90c327" :question/math-academy-id "q-351160" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,0⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}-2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-35fe18d9-346e-5613-a2e9-e0356df52875" "answer-field-f16ea21d-f232-5a8c-ae03-fff1dd1ddc4f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35fe18d9-346e-5613-a2e9-e0356df52875" :answer-field/id #uuid "35fe18d9-346e-5613-a2e9-e0356df52875" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4c630aef-e5a0-5435-bc55-02515d7f328e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c630aef-e5a0-5435-bc55-02515d7f328e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c630aef-e5a0-5435-bc55-02515d7f328e" :answer/id #uuid "4c630aef-e5a0-5435-bc55-02515d7f328e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f16ea21d-f232-5a8c-ae03-fff1dd1ddc4f" :answer-field/id #uuid "f16ea21d-f232-5a8c-ae03-fff1dd1ddc4f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-96145c9c-17f6-5fc5-8bc3-7eda2b993795" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96145c9c-17f6-5fc5-8bc3-7eda2b993795"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96145c9c-17f6-5fc5-8bc3-7eda2b993795" :answer/id #uuid "96145c9c-17f6-5fc5-8bc3-7eda2b993795" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e^{\\pi/2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e49b201b-78f0-5668-9145-a0f21b5a41c8" :question/id #uuid "e49b201b-78f0-5668-9145-a0f21b5a41c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-351222" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨6t,\\,4t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $r=⟨-12,\\,-8⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times when the particle is at the origin. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the particle is at the origin when $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=⟨0,0⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t,\\,4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 6t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-12,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2(0)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-12,-8⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-12$ and ${C}_{2}=-8.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-12,\\,2{t}^{2}-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=⟨0,0⟩.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3{t}^{2}-12,\\,2{t}^{2}-8⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12 & =3(t-2)(t+2)=0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 2, \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-8 & =2(t-2)(t+2)=0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the particle is at the origin when $t=-2$ and $t=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a97684fb-8cc0-5f02-9df1-855ab912afe7" "answer-field-4102c924-5446-5636-aa75-3bbf2bddd5bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a97684fb-8cc0-5f02-9df1-855ab912afe7" :answer-field/id #uuid "a97684fb-8cc0-5f02-9df1-855ab912afe7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0173d36c-b8c7-54e9