nswer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Z(s-6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7cabfa43-7e7e-5ad6-b175-6e755f0483d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cabfa43-7e7e-5ad6-b175-6e755f0483d0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6ef70dcf-e606-53ff-8df7-bffa2b7424c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b672e66d-dabc-5d0d-baf3-f5af944298be" "answer-6ef70dcf-e606-53ff-8df7-bffa2b7424c1" "answer-4a2b7e1a-e0b5-5856-8724-4d28931f4157"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b672e66d-dabc-5d0d-baf3-f5af944298be" :answer/id #uuid "b672e66d-dabc-5d0d-baf3-f5af944298be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ef70dcf-e606-53ff-8df7-bffa2b7424c1" :answer/id #uuid "6ef70dcf-e606-53ff-8df7-bffa2b7424c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a2b7e1a-e0b5-5856-8724-4d28931f4157" :answer/id #uuid "4a2b7e1a-e0b5-5856-8724-4d28931f4157" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a295f504-6868-5fd6-8219-b8135f071992"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a295f504-6868-5fd6-8219-b8135f071992" :question/id #uuid "a295f504-6868-5fd6-8219-b8135f071992" :question/math-academy-id "q-332061" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{6t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{6t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the right." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-07472d6e-7c20-5717-a8e5-c9592eadbbb0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-07472d6e-7c20-5717-a8e5-c9592eadbbb0" :answer-field/id #uuid "07472d6e-7c20-5717-a8e5-c9592eadbbb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ab5d9892-a999-54fb-b2bc-6e3cfce7f088" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c3fa65d-99cc-5c77-bd71-1f41ffaf620f" "answer-b0c4c853-8f85-580a-b3fc-4912e7fb4cda" "answer-1b2047a0-77e5-59eb-b2d8-c4830bf40585" "answer-ab5d9892-a999-54fb-b2bc-6e3cfce7f088" "answer-f45c8098-fbed-5d8d-a23a-ce5636def254"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c3fa65d-99cc-5c77-bd71-1f41ffaf620f" :answer/id #uuid "5c3fa65d-99cc-5c77-bd71-1f41ffaf620f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $6$ horizontally." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0c4c853-8f85-580a-b3fc-4912e7fb4cda" :answer/id #uuid "b0c4c853-8f85-580a-b3fc-4912e7fb4cda" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $6$ vertically." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b2047a0-77e5-59eb-b2d8-c4830bf40585" :answer/id #uuid "1b2047a0-77e5-59eb-b2d8-c4830bf40585" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab5d9892-a999-54fb-b2bc-6e3cfce7f088" :answer/id #uuid "ab5d9892-a999-54fb-b2bc-6e3cfce7f088" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the right." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f45c8098-fbed-5d8d-a23a-ce5636def254" :answer/id #uuid "f45c8098-fbed-5d8d-a23a-ce5636def254" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f0cfce06-cdd0-55ee-a1fd-d19a40d86dbe" :activity/id #uuid "f0cfce06-cdd0-55ee-a1fd-d19a40d86dbe" :activity/title "The First Shifting Theorem of Laplace Transforms" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050615 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-cece9bbe-7f93-5417-a941-680f66b0cf57" "step-d715b5d0-cb33-5478-a9fe-0e3da862463c" "step-b0e901f2-b8a2-54b2-9949-4ac2f8de516d" "step-b1d2ef25-b3ce-50d5-96e8-d900a85497fc" "step-a11ab2b3-b4ab-5878-9174-f53555687bd5"] :activity/first-step "step-cece9bbe-7f93-5417-a941-680f66b0cf57" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-cece9bbe-7f93-5417-a941-680f66b0cf57" :step/id #uuid "cece9bbe-7f93-5417-a941-680f66b0cf57" :step/content 17592186222044 :step/next "step-d715b5d0-cb33-5478-a9fe-0e3da862463c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d715b5d0-cb33-5478-a9fe-0e3da862463c" :step/id #uuid "d715b5d0-cb33-5478-a9fe-0e3da862463c" :step/content "question-f860a2b3-6e2b-5a0c-99c8-165c277a9c9f" :step/next "step-b0e901f2-b8a2-54b2-9949-4ac2f8de516d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b0e901f2-b8a2-54b2-9949-4ac2f8de516d" :step/id #uuid "b0e901f2-b8a2-54b2-9949-4ac2f8de516d" :step/content 17592186441316 :step/next "step-b1d2ef25-b3ce-50d5-96e8-d900a85497fc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b1d2ef25-b3ce-50d5-96e8-d900a85497fc" :step/id #uuid "b1d2ef25-b3ce-50d5-96e8-d900a85497fc" :step/content 17592186304979 :step/next "step-a11ab2b3-b4ab-5878-9174-f53555687bd5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a11ab2b3-b4ab-5878-9174-f53555687bd5" :step/id #uuid "a11ab2b3-b4ab-5878-9174-f53555687bd5" :step/content "question-a295f504-6868-5fd6-8219-b8135f071992" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083340 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3a25fcb4-6a85-5416-8ab8-d6107a3d8b17" "question-30cdb164-81fa-5575-aede-39cb02771ca3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3a25fcb4-6a85-5416-8ab8-d6107a3d8b17" :question/id #uuid "3a25fcb4-6a85-5416-8ab8-d6107a3d8b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-6022" :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\cdot {n}^{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{n-1/2}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d3052d66-0697-5930-85f9-c584087760e2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d3052d66-0697-5930-85f9-c584087760e2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3052d66-0697-5930-85f9-c584087760e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0b64790-21c2-5f37-b2cc-33801393cf8c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0b64790-21c2-5f37-b2cc-33801393cf8c" "answer-ce352136-40a5-50fe-a545-036a2a82efb5" "answer-3fe33770-f073-58ae-98ff-6550e980a4f5" "answer-4c4501bf-5593-532c-b1be-a42d350ce985" "answer-b18e1a27-a6d5-5ac9-936a-fb892b199757"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0b64790-21c2-5f37-b2cc-33801393cf8c" :answer/id #uuid "e0b64790-21c2-5f37-b2cc-33801393cf8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=0.$ The series is convergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce352136-40a5-50fe-a545-036a2a82efb5" :answer/id #uuid "ce352136-40a5-50fe-a545-036a2a82efb5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=3.$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fe33770-f073-58ae-98ff-6550e980a4f5" :answer/id #uuid "3fe33770-f073-58ae-98ff-6550e980a4f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{1}{3}.$ The series is convergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c4501bf-5593-532c-b1be-a42d350ce985" :answer/id #uuid "4c4501bf-5593-532c-b1be-a42d350ce985" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{1}{9}.$ The series is convergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b18e1a27-a6d5-5ac9-936a-fb892b199757" :answer/id #uuid "b18e1a27-a6d5-5ac9-936a-fb892b199757" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\infty .$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-30cdb164-81fa-5575-aede-39cb02771ca3" :question/id #uuid "30cdb164-81fa-5575-aede-39cb02771ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-6027" :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.$ Does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{11}{7\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n!}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d64a5913-6f1a-5c6e-88a9-5daa0337d7b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d64a5913-6f1a-5c6e-88a9-5daa0337d7b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "d64a5913-6f1a-5c6e-88a9-5daa0337d7b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a9c65a76-4ede-5228-964c-67fc95c51b32" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9c65a76-4ede-5228-964c-67fc95c51b32" "answer-47795a6f-f09c-5ce0-8648-0dda3b3866d2" "answer-59da41e9-24a6-5453-ad33-2fd47886a1f6" "answer-db748b8f-576e-5783-80b0-1618e62a128d" "answer-d58740e8-ed0f-5cea-b77a-0df6635012b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9c65a76-4ede-5228-964c-67fc95c51b32" :answer/id #uuid "a9c65a76-4ede-5228-964c-67fc95c51b32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=0.$ The series is convergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47795a6f-f09c-5ce0-8648-0dda3b3866d2" :answer/id #uuid "47795a6f-f09c-5ce0-8648-0dda3b3866d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{11}{7}.$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59da41e9-24a6-5453-ad33-2fd47886a1f6" :answer/id #uuid "59da41e9-24a6-5453-ad33-2fd47886a1f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{1}{49}.$ The series is convergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db748b8f-576e-5783-80b0-1618e62a128d" :answer/id #uuid "db748b8f-576e-5783-80b0-1618e62a128d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{1}{7}.$ The series is convergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d58740e8-ed0f-5cea-b77a-0df6635012b7" :answer/id #uuid "d58740e8-ed0f-5cea-b77a-0df6635012b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\infty .$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083341 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0f1fd02c-d7d8-5411-85bd-473d05c64e92" "question-d497f3f8-01e4-568d-82c0-af0842ace04d" "question-af92a5d6-20ee-53fa-be9c-28e2a23392fd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f1fd02c-d7d8-5411-85bd-473d05c64e92" :question/id #uuid "0f1fd02c-d7d8-5411-85bd-473d05c64e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-14951" :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n}\\cdot n}{(9+5n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{9+5n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\cdot \\frac{5n}{n})}{(\\frac{9}{n}+\\frac{5n}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(\\frac{9}{n}+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1}{0+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f5cf7d5d-3952-5581-beaf-679fd852cabd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f5cf7d5d-3952-5581-beaf-679fd852cabd" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5cf7d5d-3952-5581-beaf-679fd852cabd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7c46e210-d2d6-55e4-892f-4ca85507253f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7c46e210-d2d6-55e4-892f-4ca85507253f" "answer-0b0efef7-ea22-56c7-ac43-7422add3b46d" "answer-a5779aa3-3102-5b67-aa38-f2aff312d456" "answer-4f4a5642-c9e9-5168-8482-b21e873612c5" "answer-09a5894b-ca93-559e-9241-c70e09cfcc05"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c46e210-d2d6-55e4-892f-4ca85507253f" :answer/id #uuid "7c46e210-d2d6-55e4-892f-4ca85507253f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=1.$ The root test gives no conclusion." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b0efef7-ea22-56c7-ac43-7422add3b46d" :answer/id #uuid "0b0efef7-ea22-56c7-ac43-7422add3b46d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=1.$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5779aa3-3102-5b67-aa38-f2aff312d456" :answer/id #uuid "a5779aa3-3102-5b67-aa38-f2aff312d456" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=5.$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f4a5642-c9e9-5168-8482-b21e873612c5" :answer/id #uuid "4f4a5642-c9e9-5168-8482-b21e873612c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{1}{9}.$ The series is convergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09a5894b-ca93-559e-9241-c70e09cfcc05" :answer/id #uuid "09a5894b-ca93-559e-9241-c70e09cfcc05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\infty .$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d497f3f8-01e4-568d-82c0-af0842ace04d" :question/id #uuid "d497f3f8-01e4-568d-82c0-af0842ace04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14954" :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6}{5\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{{n}^{6}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{n}})}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {1}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{6}{5}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88aae151-d533-504a-94e6-0bbe62cec52b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88aae151-d533-504a-94e6-0bbe62cec52b" :answer-field/id #uuid "88aae151-d533-504a-94e6-0bbe62cec52b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0a6ce84e-109c-502d-ae75-00961ce9ff86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a01506fd-1477-5630-b72b-044bad1929cb" "answer-667a0c54-51d8-5adb-87dd-0040efd8d40d" "answer-c8f6e974-a325-5bc8-805b-63f27f6b2359" "answer-0a6ce84e-109c-502d-ae75-00961ce9ff86" "answer-50b57e3f-e86f-5664-a7b4-68a0717a38e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a01506fd-1477-5630-b72b-044bad1929cb" :answer/id #uuid "a01506fd-1477-5630-b72b-044bad1929cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=1.$ The root test gives no conclusion." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-667a0c54-51d8-5adb-87dd-0040efd8d40d" :answer/id #uuid "667a0c54-51d8-5adb-87dd-0040efd8d40d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=6.$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8f6e974-a325-5bc8-805b-63f27f6b2359" :answer/id #uuid "c8f6e974-a325-5bc8-805b-63f27f6b2359" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{5}{6}.$ The series is convergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a6ce84e-109c-502d-ae75-00961ce9ff86" :answer/id #uuid "0a6ce84e-109c-502d-ae75-00961ce9ff86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{6}{5}.$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50b57e3f-e86f-5664-a7b4-68a0717a38e2" :answer/id #uuid "50b57e3f-e86f-5664-a7b4-68a0717a38e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\infty .$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-af92a5d6-20ee-53fa-be9c-28e2a23392fd" :question/id #uuid "af92a5d6-20ee-53fa-be9c-28e2a23392fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-139038" :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{8n+1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{n(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{8+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{5}{4}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ff32e85-d602-5a3e-9ef1-a2d2944e6ae0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ff32e85-d602-5a3e-9ef1-a2d2944e6ae0" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ff32e85-d602-5a3e-9ef1-a2d2944e6ae0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c253c48-5507-56d5-9451-af7ca3386fc0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9c49c143-d35a-594e-8e97-d80bb0c9ac82" "answer-c5e371ec-a315-5c84-89a5-b0df5f32e35f" "answer-6199dc51-e96e-5636-83f2-2579cabce364" "answer-6c253c48-5507-56d5-9451-af7ca3386fc0" "answer-73785847-2f06-56d2-b76c-2efa341aaff5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c49c143-d35a-594e-8e97-d80bb0c9ac82" :answer/id #uuid "9c49c143-d35a-594e-8e97-d80bb0c9ac82" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=1.$ The root test gives no conclusion." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5e371ec-a315-5c84-89a5-b0df5f32e35f" :answer/id #uuid "c5e371ec-a315-5c84-89a5-b0df5f32e35f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=10.$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6199dc51-e96e-5636-83f2-2579cabce364" :answer/id #uuid "6199dc51-e96e-5636-83f2-2579cabce364" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{1}{8}.$ The series is convergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c253c48-5507-56d5-9451-af7ca3386fc0" :answer/id #uuid "6c253c48-5507-56d5-9451-af7ca3386fc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\frac{5}{4}.$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73785847-2f06-56d2-b76c-2efa341aaff5" :answer/id #uuid "73785847-2f06-56d2-b76c-2efa341aaff5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$L=\\infty .$ The series is divergent." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-4bb58ead-0937-504b-94e2-53c622c58693" :activity/id #uuid "4bb58ead-0937-504b-94e2-53c622c58693" :activity/title "The Root Test" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050957 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-b9351eac-8615-5d5f-97f7-1246fb7f128b" "step-c7f6c44c-a20c-524b-85ce-89426189d976" "step-8979fcef-655b-5dad-8545-0b736d277a09" "step-39e29671-6707-57da-9854-b346b8c2d709" "step-114b601e-a2a6-516f-83df-76e39dceef0a"] :activity/first-step "step-b9351eac-8615-5d5f-97f7-1246fb7f128b" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-b9351eac-8615-5d5f-97f7-1246fb7f128b" :step/id #uuid "b9351eac-8615-5d5f-97f7-1246fb7f128b" :step/content "question-3a25fcb4-6a85-5416-8ab8-d6107a3d8b17" :step/next "step-c7f6c44c-a20c-524b-85ce-89426189d976" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c7f6c44c-a20c-524b-85ce-89426189d976" :step/id #uuid "c7f6c44c-a20c-524b-85ce-89426189d976" :step/content "question-30cdb164-81fa-5575-aede-39cb02771ca3" :step/next "step-8979fcef-655b-5dad-8545-0b736d277a09" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8979fcef-655b-5dad-8545-0b736d277a09" :step/id #uuid "8979fcef-655b-5dad-8545-0b736d277a09" :step/content "question-0f1fd02c-d7d8-5411-85bd-473d05c64e92" :step/next "step-39e29671-6707-57da-9854-b346b8c2d709" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-39e29671-6707-57da-9854-b346b8c2d709" :step/id #uuid "39e29671-6707-57da-9854-b346b8c2d709" :step/content "question-d497f3f8-01e4-568d-82c0-af0842ace04d" :step/next "step-114b601e-a2a6-516f-83df-76e39dceef0a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-114b601e-a2a6-516f-83df-76e39dceef0a" :step/id #uuid "114b601e-a2a6-516f-83df-76e39dceef0a" :step/content "question-af92a5d6-20ee-53fa-be9c-28e2a23392fd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-52c6a4cd-b903-5d26-bd7b-6413b0fd0ae1" "question-725799d2-b847-5f90-a1b3-5a3cdfe95070" "question-4bbaa29d-7536-57fa-abdb-fc58099b0936" "question-d4c5ef62-20c9-5f14-af52-91dedd4c1fc7" "question-b62ef58e-043d-550b-83fb-b95a07d27915"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068553 :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $\\Delta x,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}=\\dfrac{2-0}{4}=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our situation. We are using a left Riemann sum, which means we draw the rectangles so that the left corner of each rectangle coincides with the curve.\n\n![](images/56/56427bec8108b822c4e1980725073cedc9fc79575d606014f72ea6ca9f8959c2.png)\n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A≈(16+9+4+1)\\cdot 0.5=15\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f3cd5193-df6c-5672-95df-52f56264b423" "question-84844930-f95f-5f3a-8037-4adc201a50fc" "question-fd327109-8d69-58f0-a86a-7b3b70080707" "question-9be6577c-8d1d-5823-8ae8-eea53999e09f" "question-247e80ec-6b94-56ac-94f4-78dcaa13b56f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068640 :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[-4,2].$ If the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $28,$ then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula for the left Riemann sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(-4)}}+{\\color{red}{f(-2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(5+3+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(8+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $\\mathcal A$ must be $28,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+2k & =28 \\\\ 2k & =12 \\\\ k & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2a6ffc0a-296f-5d33-ab74-d79dec06ff68" "question-b8274948-1d4b-57b0-9024-c09c00401038" "question-8bc09582-a4d5-5866-bf69-244d81d8d845" "question-7e9c9afd-571f-54f0-b5de-d6a5e5729d5e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068641 :question/problem "Suppose a left Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)=(2x-4)^2$ over the interval $[0,2].$ Is the resulting estimation an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation. We are using a left Riemann sum, which means we draw the rectangles so that the left corner of each rectangle coincides with the curve.\n\n![](images/02/02b3449f0ccdb2f60eb489aec1b34485ee381b644a52eea7f5394e16851b6cff.png)\n\nBecause the function is *decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e9dcd5b8-f37b-55de-ab7a-6d8ec569a890" "question-7303a5f4-0c27-5036-a2ee-f35a42950785" "question-991c1ace-0aaf-57b9-84aa-e45b1d07c8fd" "question-79154f2c-c86a-5c07-9c5a-51aae4f7090e" "question-6dc2cb61-7e14-5e92-9670-4b835f928971"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068554 :question/problem "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,2].$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.8$ | $1.6$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is computed as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}\t\\approx\tf(0.0)\\Delta x_1+f(0.8)\\Delta x_2+f(1.6)\\Delta x_3,\n$$\n\nwhere each $\\Delta x_i$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\Delta x_i = x_{i+1}-x_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $\\Delta x$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.8$ | $1.6$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Delta{x}$ | $0.8$ | $0.8$ | $0.4$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈2\\cdot 0.8+5\\cdot 0.8+10\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.6+4+4 \\\\  & =9.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-52c6a4cd-b903-5d26-bd7b-6413b0fd0ae1" :question/id #uuid "52c6a4cd-b903-5d26-bd7b-6413b0fd0ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-364487" :question/problem "Estimate the area $A$ under the curve $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/27/275b62fbec1c1a550cb23b481ec54c1e12d3d42e70cf95cd917f1b932638add4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5))\\cdot 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-1$ at each left endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $8$ | $15$ | $24$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+8+15+24)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-60816e31-a460-50bd-9564-d4219818a430"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60816e31-a460-50bd-9564-d4219818a430" :answer-field/id #uuid "60816e31-a460-50bd-9564-d4219818a430" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1314f4b3-9571-556b-ad75-81e6e271fd2f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1314f4b3-9571-556b-ad75-81e6e271fd2f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1314f4b3-9571-556b-ad75-81e6e271fd2f" :answer/id #uuid "1314f4b3-9571-556b-ad75-81e6e271fd2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-725799d2-b847-5f90-a1b3-5a3cdfe95070" :question/id #uuid "725799d2-b847-5f90-a1b3-5a3cdfe95070" :question/math-academy-id "q-19890" :question/problem "A speedboat travels downstream on a river. The table below gives the speed $v(t)$, in miles per hour, of the boat at certain moments during its journey.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{mi/hour})$ | $10$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ | $80$ |\n\nUse a left Riemann sum for $v(t)$ over the interval $[0,2]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total distance traveled by the speedboat.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-0}{4}=0.5.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v({x}_{0})+v({t}_{1})+…+v({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(v(0)+v(0.5)+v(1)+v(1.5))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $10$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(10+35+40+65)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =150\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled by the speedboat. So, the distance is $75\\,\\text{mi}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b71fa70-bd24-5e39-8659-a1858750b9fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b71fa70-bd24-5e39-8659-a1858750b9fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b71fa70-bd24-5e39-8659-a1858750b9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a014c8ae-f0bd-5ff7-a1e7-9e3747de7adf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4d6386f-3f09-5d69-8338-27e6df95af4d" "answer-b23a1ed8-832f-51a4-9037-8703bcf7ddc6" "answer-9fc50b29-7baf-5a27-96d5-142128ec93ab" "answer-a014c8ae-f0bd-5ff7-a1e7-9e3747de7adf" "answer-d4a87ab8-97db-50f2-b37e-b52d8b7bc07a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4d6386f-3f09-5d69-8338-27e6df95af4d" :answer/id #uuid "d4d6386f-3f09-5d69-8338-27e6df95af4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "110\\,\\text{mi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b23a1ed8-832f-51a4-9037-8703bcf7ddc6" :answer/id #uuid "b23a1ed8-832f-51a4-9037-8703bcf7ddc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "115\\,\\text{mi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fc50b29-7baf-5a27-96d5-142128ec93ab" :answer/id #uuid "9fc50b29-7baf-5a27-96d5-142128ec93ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "65\\,\\text{mi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a014c8ae-f0bd-5ff7-a1e7-9e3747de7adf" :answer/id #uuid "a014c8ae-f0bd-5ff7-a1e7-9e3747de7adf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "75\\,\\text{mi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4a87ab8-97db-50f2-b37e-b52d8b7bc07a" :answer/id #uuid "d4a87ab8-97db-50f2-b37e-b52d8b7bc07a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80\\,\\text{mi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4bbaa29d-7536-57fa-abdb-fc58099b0936" :question/id #uuid "4bbaa29d-7536-57fa-abdb-fc58099b0936" :question/math-academy-id "q-86288" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,10]$ with a regular step size. Estimate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,10]$ using a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(4)+f(7))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the interval $[1,10]$ is divided into $3$ equal subintervals, the width of each subinterval is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{10-1}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the left endpoints:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+0)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4f5a1c82-2697-5ad0-92be-ffc88f085f93"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4f5a1c82-2697-5ad0-92be-ffc88f085f93" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f5a1c82-2697-5ad0-92be-ffc88f085f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2241ebaf-ee5e-55f8-ae67-45d4013fe205" :answer-field/choices ["answer-185282f2-7ff6-5c6b-b9b9-e4a2049ff490" "answer-1a540d1d-50f6-5433-a351-e20154df9816" "answer-4a2d11e1-346f-5678-87b0-f47869751fb1" "answer-9abf62e4-c1ec-5172-86a6-3a5ad254967f" "answer-2241ebaf-ee5e-55f8-ae67-45d4013fe205"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-185282f2-7ff6-5c6b-b9b9-e4a2049ff490" :answer/id #uuid "185282f2-7ff6-5c6b-b9b9-e4a2049ff490" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a540d1d-50f6-5433-a351-e20154df9816" :answer/id #uuid "1a540d1d-50f6-5433-a351-e20154df9816" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a2d11e1-346f-5678-87b0-f47869751fb1" :answer/id #uuid "4a2d11e1-346f-5678-87b0-f47869751fb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9abf62e4-c1ec-5172-86a6-3a5ad254967f" :answer/id #uuid "9abf62e4-c1ec-5172-86a6-3a5ad254967f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2241ebaf-ee5e-55f8-ae67-45d4013fe205" :answer/id #uuid "2241ebaf-ee5e-55f8-ae67-45d4013fe205" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d4c5ef62-20c9-5f14-af52-91dedd4c1fc7" :question/id #uuid "d4c5ef62-20c9-5f14-af52-91dedd4c1fc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115676" :question/problem "A tank is filling with soda. The table below gives the rate $R(t)$, in liters per minute, at which the tank is filling at certain moments since it started.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{liters/minute})$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ | $90$ |\n\nGiven that the tank is empty when $t=0,$ use a left Riemann sum for $R(t)$ over the interval $[0,12]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total volume of soda in the tank after $12$ minutes." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=12,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{12-0}{4}=3.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R({t}_{0})+R({t}_{1})+…+R({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(R(0)+R(3)+R(6)+R(9))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $R(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(30+40+60+70)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =200\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total volume in the tank. So, the volume is $600$ liters." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-33c6bcac-1b81-5ea4-b234-754b353a54a7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-33c6bcac-1b81-5ea4-b234-754b353a54a7" :answer-field/id #uuid "33c6bcac-1b81-5ea4-b234-754b353a54a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ebdf27a-4c72-5cb8-aa57-5a63e946d3bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9602873-3dbc-505a-b6a6-91eb730e5c49" "answer-65cd7e27-fab6-576d-9c25-07914a986223" "answer-0da9d2c8-f87f-52ab-bc1e-affb04efcc9b" "answer-0ebdf27a-4c72-5cb8-aa57-5a63e946d3bc" "answer-3caddbf5-505a-5c83-800d-32cdc8dd40d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9602873-3dbc-505a-b6a6-91eb730e5c49" :answer/id #uuid "e9602873-3dbc-505a-b6a6-91eb730e5c49" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$10\\,000$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65cd7e27-fab6-576d-9c25-07914a986223" :answer/id #uuid "65cd7e27-fab6-576d-9c25-07914a986223" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2\\,000$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0da9d2c8-f87f-52ab-bc1e-affb04efcc9b" :answer/id #uuid "0da9d2c8-f87f-52ab-bc1e-affb04efcc9b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$400$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ebdf27a-4c72-5cb8-aa57-5a63e946d3bc" :answer/id #uuid "0ebdf27a-4c72-5cb8-aa57-5a63e946d3bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$600$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3caddbf5-505a-5c83-800d-32cdc8dd40d0" :answer/id #uuid "3caddbf5-505a-5c83-800d-32cdc8dd40d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$800$ liters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b62ef58e-043d-550b-83fb-b95a07d27915" :question/id #uuid "b62ef58e-043d-550b-83fb-b95a07d27915" :question/math-academy-id "q-74490" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0.5,2.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/0e/0e3f499cbc36c9dca7e759b67a8fd9e94959039f3403832e1d60b9ed598c44dd.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $2.25$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1.25+2+2.25+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa9732c7-7fe4-5d54-92ce-d54efeda2989"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa9732c7-7fe4-5d54-92ce-d54efeda2989" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9732c7-7fe4-5d54-92ce-d54efeda2989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-729df441-7198-586b-a2e8-ab6ac0c01a83" :answer-field/choices ["answer-efdc7fbe-3706-5051-a3b0-a9413406ae28" "answer-729df441-7198-586b-a2e8-ab6ac0c01a83" "answer-210cb1e7-4d4f-521d-94ea-b92ddb31ec30" "answer-e7dec5bb-a6e8-5021-8f8f-827f57c20855" "answer-a1f94a57-31fa-5cc6-ae1d-146b8abaa792"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efdc7fbe-3706-5051-a3b0-a9413406ae28" :answer/id #uuid "efdc7fbe-3706-5051-a3b0-a9413406ae28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.57" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-729df441-7198-586b-a2e8-ab6ac0c01a83" :answer/id #uuid "729df441-7198-586b-a2e8-ab6ac0c01a83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-210cb1e7-4d4f-521d-94ea-b92ddb31ec30" :answer/id #uuid "210cb1e7-4d4f-521d-94ea-b92ddb31ec30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7dec5bb-a6e8-5021-8f8f-827f57c20855" :answer/id #uuid "e7dec5bb-a6e8-5021-8f8f-827f57c20855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1f94a57-31fa-5cc6-ae1d-146b8abaa792" :answer/id #uuid "a1f94a57-31fa-5cc6-ae1d-146b8abaa792" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.53" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f3cd5193-df6c-5672-95df-52f56264b423" :question/id #uuid "f3cd5193-df6c-5672-95df-52f56264b423" :question/math-academy-id "q-115688" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[-3,3]$ with a regular step size. If the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $4$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(-3)+f(-1)+f(1))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be $4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d71bc7f8-343e-57a7-96e4-46b5c22b0949"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d71bc7f8-343e-57a7-96e4-46b5c22b0949" :answer-field/id #uuid "d71bc7f8-343e-57a7-96e4-46b5c22b0949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-52b56205-1df3-51bc-8170-ccb928677b3b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9681dbd2-1094-52d5-b2bd-83e405cca6a9" "answer-45fc19c2-908b-50ae-ba05-376e1cbaa786" "answer-52b56205-1df3-51bc-8170-ccb928677b3b" "answer-6f8030cb-2dd5-50f4-a3eb-3a5316c29433" "answer-0960c82b-6c1b-5b1f-8bf9-41ec598d133a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9681dbd2-1094-52d5-b2bd-83e405cca6a9" :answer/id #uuid "9681dbd2-1094-52d5-b2bd-83e405cca6a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45fc19c2-908b-50ae-ba05-376e1cbaa786" :answer/id #uuid "45fc19c2-908b-50ae-ba05-376e1cbaa786" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52b56205-1df3-51bc-8170-ccb928677b3b" :answer/id #uuid "52b56205-1df3-51bc-8170-ccb928677b3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f8030cb-2dd5-50f4-a3eb-3a5316c29433" :answer/id #uuid "6f8030cb-2dd5-50f4-a3eb-3a5316c29433" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0960c82b-6c1b-5b1f-8bf9-41ec598d133a" :answer/id #uuid "0960c82b-6c1b-5b1f-8bf9-41ec598d133a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-84844930-f95f-5f3a-8037-4adc201a50fc" :question/id #uuid "84844930-f95f-5f3a-8037-4adc201a50fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86298" :question/problem "The following table shows the rate of change of a city's temperature over a $10$-hour period, starting at $2\\,\\text{a.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{h})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T\\,{(}^{∘}\\text{C}/\\text{h})$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}$ has a value of $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=T(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{T({t}_{0})+T({t}_{1})+…+T({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(T(2)+T(4)+T(6)+T(8)+T(10))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $T(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+10) \\\\  & =2k+20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature is $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+20 & =26 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e817f9ef-3f03-51be-bb7b-0bc236498520"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e817f9ef-3f03-51be-bb7b-0bc236498520" :answer-field/id #uuid "e817f9ef-3f03-51be-bb7b-0bc236498520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-89cc52d0-7d13-5b28-b988-28ce44b1ddc9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6776dc08-9fdf-51fd-996a-bfab15c3331d" "answer-228eec43-938a-5953-916c-999c2cbbaa37" "answer-89cc52d0-7d13-5b28-b988-28ce44b1ddc9" "answer-a159d0cd-4dba-5b36-b32e-909f05583e6d" "answer-9d78173e-a4fa-5da6-a57c-b9e952b87134"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6776dc08-9fdf-51fd-996a-bfab15c3331d" :answer/id #uuid "6776dc08-9fdf-51fd-996a-bfab15c3331d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-228eec43-938a-5953-916c-999c2cbbaa37" :answer/id #uuid "228eec43-938a-5953-916c-999c2cbbaa37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89cc52d0-7d13-5b28-b988-28ce44b1ddc9" :answer/id #uuid "89cc52d0-7d13-5b28-b988-28ce44b1ddc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a159d0cd-4dba-5b36-b32e-909f05583e6d" :answer/id #uuid "a159d0cd-4dba-5b36-b32e-909f05583e6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d78173e-a4fa-5da6-a57c-b9e952b87134" :answer/id #uuid "9d78173e-a4fa-5da6-a57c-b9e952b87134" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd327109-8d69-58f0-a86a-7b3b70080707" :question/id #uuid "fd327109-8d69-58f0-a86a-7b3b70080707" :question/math-academy-id "q-86296" :question/problem "John is selling juice at a market stall. The table below gives the rate $r(t)$ of juice sold over a period of $6$ hours, from $1\\,\\text{p.m.}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m.}$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,(\\text{liters/hour})$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters John sold during the given period has a value of $16$ liters, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(1)+r(3)+r(5))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+5) \\\\  & =2k+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by John. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $16,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+10 & =16 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-63889206-4140-5315-b206-7bddd19df74c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-63889206-4140-5315-b206-7bddd19df74c" :answer-field/id #uuid "63889206-4140-5315-b206-7bddd19df74c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5b64cde6-94d5-505a-acf7-2a0725fed336" :answer-field/choices ["answer-959c80c5-314d-5889-8b74-145a524b94ae" "answer-74ee3ad3-6f87-5264-ad1c-f19f7f542b25" "answer-5b64cde6-94d5-505a-acf7-2a0725fed336" "answer-02a2b37e-f7fd-5fb0-af6d-9e771b691d02" "answer-732c7367-4e22-5a0a-8514-ecf1b997bfbc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-959c80c5-314d-5889-8b74-145a524b94ae" :answer/id #uuid "959c80c5-314d-5889-8b74-145a524b94ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74ee3ad3-6f87-5264-ad1c-f19f7f542b25" :answer/id #uuid "74ee3ad3-6f87-5264-ad1c-f19f7f542b25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b64cde6-94d5-505a-acf7-2a0725fed336" :answer/id #uuid "5b64cde6-94d5-505a-acf7-2a0725fed336" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02a2b37e-f7fd-5fb0-af6d-9e771b691d02" :answer/id #uuid "02a2b37e-f7fd-5fb0-af6d-9e771b691d02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-732c7367-4e22-5a0a-8514-ecf1b997bfbc" :answer/id #uuid "732c7367-4e22-5a0a-8514-ecf1b997bfbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9be6577c-8d1d-5823-8ae8-eea53999e09f" :question/id #uuid "9be6577c-8d1d-5823-8ae8-eea53999e09f" :question/math-academy-id "q-315694" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[2,8]$ with a regular step size.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $18$, then what must be the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(2)+f(4)+f(6))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the points are $2,4,6,8$ with three subintervals, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{8-2}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each left-endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈((-3)+5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A=18$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2k & =18 \\\\ 2k & =14 \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-094a080c-b2c0-5da9-a43a-31f0cd0dae34"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-094a080c-b2c0-5da9-a43a-31f0cd0dae34" :answer-field/id #uuid "094a080c-b2c0-5da9-a43a-31f0cd0dae34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-556a6e29-9fdc-550d-b85c-3606a717e3f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-556a6e29-9fdc-550d-b85c-3606a717e3f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-556a6e29-9fdc-550d-b85c-3606a717e3f5" :answer/id #uuid "556a6e29-9fdc-550d-b85c-3606a717e3f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-247e80ec-6b94-56ac-94f4-78dcaa13b56f" :question/id #uuid "247e80ec-6b94-56ac-94f4-78dcaa13b56f" :question/math-academy-id "q-315696" :question/problem "Maya runs a lemonade stand. The table below gives the selling rate $r(t)$ (in liters per hour) over a $6$-hour period starting at noon.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,(\\text{L/hour})$ | $1$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold over the $6$-hour period (with three subintervals defined by the given points) is $28$ liters, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(0)+r(2)+r(4))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by Maya. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $28,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+12 & =28 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4210f50-ff89-5293-88ab-8316c3879985"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4210f50-ff89-5293-88ab-8316c3879985" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4210f50-ff89-5293-88ab-8316c3879985" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e6f68175-b7da-5e97-b323-b3ecf7706e24" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e6f68175-b7da-5e97-b323-b3ecf7706e24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6f68175-b7da-5e97-b323-b3ecf7706e24" :answer/id #uuid "e6f68175-b7da-5e97-b323-b3ecf7706e24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069376 :question/problem "A left Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-1$ and $x=3$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *overestimate* of the area?" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum overestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is decreasing over $x\\in [-1,3]$ is the following. \n\n![](images/62/622f8fd49fb32255b29bd0ae8c36c146aeb4b48aec7be2cde69832cb33fd2d83.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186067426 :answer-field/correct 17592186067431 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-2a6ffc0a-296f-5d33-ab74-d79dec06ff68" :question/id #uuid "2a6ffc0a-296f-5d33-ab74-d79dec06ff68" :question/math-academy-id "q-346698" :question/problem "Consider the area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=1.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/671d5efc8abc559cf638bfbfdda76bddb0b8d5c2d9134dffbbf7eab0e2f0b25a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $7$ | $17$ | $31$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+7+17+31)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[1,5],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-228f7032-0430-5206-88b5-0be9f2a8574f" "answer-field-0e022b11-7e38-5845-8ce8-d823ff176f78" "answer-field-a63a1ae7-518a-536a-919b-d5fff57d94d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-228f7032-0430-5206-88b5-0be9f2a8574f" :answer-field/id #uuid "228f7032-0430-5206-88b5-0be9f2a8574f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-e84c0fb2-d0da-5c46-9013-29649f607491" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5bfeaaed-4260-554a-aed3-a5bacbddffde" "answer-e84c0fb2-d0da-5c46-9013-29649f607491" "answer-eac4869b-ddab-5f08-9de9-9c32f40be8bb" "answer-8e5d3818-a0f9-5df3-97cf-a289e19e25f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bfeaaed-4260-554a-aed3-a5bacbddffde" :answer/id #uuid "5bfeaaed-4260-554a-aed3-a5bacbddffde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e84c0fb2-d0da-5c46-9013-29649f607491" :answer/id #uuid "e84c0fb2-d0da-5c46-9013-29649f607491" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "56" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eac4869b-ddab-5f08-9de9-9c32f40be8bb" :answer/id #uuid "eac4869b-ddab-5f08-9de9-9c32f40be8bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "58" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e5d3818-a0f9-5df3-97cf-a289e19e25f2" :answer/id #uuid "8e5d3818-a0f9-5df3-97cf-a289e19e25f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0e022b11-7e38-5845-8ce8-d823ff176f78" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e022b11-7e38-5845-8ce8-d823ff176f78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-681e5cac-8278-59c0-9cb0-0431661360f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b971d682-7baf-5c86-8f72-52ba7b01ea60" "answer-681e5cac-8278-59c0-9cb0-0431661360f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b971d682-7baf-5c86-8f72-52ba7b01ea60" :answer/id #uuid "b971d682-7baf-5c86-8f72-52ba7b01ea60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "decreasing" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-681e5cac-8278-59c0-9cb0-0431661360f8" :answer/id #uuid "681e5cac-8278-59c0-9cb0-0431661360f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "increasing" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a63a1ae7-518a-536a-919b-d5fff57d94d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a63a1ae7-518a-536a-919b-d5fff57d94d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c0127cd4-ffb2-567a-9f87-a5b7ee9a3fd8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15977689-22a7-5a1e-aa5e-b101b88ce8dd" "answer-9fe08a89-41ef-5c59-af03-7a5095196299" "answer-c0127cd4-ffb2-567a-9f87-a5b7ee9a3fd8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15977689-22a7-5a1e-aa5e-b101b88ce8dd" :answer/id #uuid "15977689-22a7-5a1e-aa5e-b101b88ce8dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "exact value" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fe08a89-41ef-5c59-af03-7a5095196299" :answer/id #uuid "9fe08a89-41ef-5c59-af03-7a5095196299" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "overestimate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0127cd4-ffb2-567a-9f87-a5b7ee9a3fd8" :answer/id #uuid "c0127cd4-ffb2-567a-9f87-a5b7ee9a3fd8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "underestimate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b8274948-1d4b-57b0-9024-c09c00401038" :question/id #uuid "b8274948-1d4b-57b0-9024-c09c00401038" :question/math-academy-id "q-115695" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+1$ over the interval $[1.5,3.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ad/ad1e85721eb2142f5cad422530aeed86c0919f34261d19e11e9f056cf355b387.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)+f(3)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.25$ | $3$ | $2.25$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.25+3+2.25+1)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =9.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[1.5,3.5],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4c68885-5ff0-588b-a710-c9a4d588c9db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4c68885-5ff0-588b-a710-c9a4d588c9db" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c68885-5ff0-588b-a710-c9a4d588c9db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-da4041b1-902c-5100-90dd-0f72405fae3b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a35d4692-5d9f-53e3-93e2-8277320977bf" "answer-2c60a466-a39a-5a5b-bb84-5d4cb7876254" "answer-da4041b1-902c-5100-90dd-0f72405fae3b" "answer-8c1b0e03-2b27-5442-9fcf-fd518b599444" "answer-9f63f2ef-7e04-5845-a994-8b3a76a5610d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a35d4692-5d9f-53e3-93e2-8277320977bf" :answer/id #uuid "a35d4692-5d9f-53e3-93e2-8277320977bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2.75,$ overestimate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c60a466-a39a-5a5b-bb84-5d4cb7876254" :answer/id #uuid "2c60a466-a39a-5a5b-bb84-5d4cb7876254" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2.75,$ underestimate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da4041b1-902c-5100-90dd-0f72405fae3b" :answer/id #uuid "da4041b1-902c-5100-90dd-0f72405fae3b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4.75,$ overestimate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c1b0e03-2b27-5442-9fcf-fd518b599444" :answer/id #uuid "8c1b0e03-2b27-5442-9fcf-fd518b599444" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4.75,$ underestimate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f63f2ef-7e04-5845-a994-8b3a76a5610d" :answer/id #uuid "9f63f2ef-7e04-5845-a994-8b3a76a5610d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5.75,$ overestimate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8bc09582-a4d5-5866-bf69-244d81d8d845" :question/id #uuid "8bc09582-a4d5-5866-bf69-244d81d8d845" :question/math-academy-id "q-74503" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x+2$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/2c/2cd664908ea218fecf5a33ea3312bd883f5495d64fc0da8387632a7d2e17c26a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $3.25$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+1.25+2+3.25)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,3],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1c4ed498-d752-509d-b505-ce28b302f4d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1c4ed498-d752-509d-b505-ce28b302f4d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c4ed498-d752-509d-b505-ce28b302f4d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ee58283-1522-5f63-854d-16362a3b4b0c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-515320ab-5f99-5b9e-8e87-3bb603a6ba82" "answer-5ee58283-1522-5f63-854d-16362a3b4b0c" "answer-83da73b0-48f3-586d-85b3-eb18d7a17400" "answer-da4e4c5a-7f58-5eea-ab10-1c79a44758d0" "answer-f2fb0583-ff57-5c40-ac23-4c564c2513d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-515320ab-5f99-5b9e-8e87-3bb603a6ba82" :answer/id #uuid "515320ab-5f99-5b